	[m1][trn]not fml[/p] откупаться (от кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ His sister threatened to tell the police, so he had to buy her off.[/ex]
	
buy out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 выкупать (всё предприятие) и становиться хозяином:[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ Mr Sharp has enough money to buy out the town.[/ex]
	→ buy up (1)
	[m1][trn]2 освободиться от какого-л. обязательства (напр., обязанности нести воинекую службу) путём уплаты определённой суммы:[/trn]
	\[[com]Т1 (of) [/com]\] [ex]♦ Bill's parents paid a lot of money to buy him out of the army.[/ex]
	
buy up
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 выкупать (всё предприятие) и становиться хозяином:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The company bought up all the other cinemas and so became free of competition.[/ex] → buy out (1)
	[m1][trn]2 скупать, закупать впрок:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Housewives, afraid of running short, have bought up all the sugar in the shops.[/ex]
	
buzz off
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ push off (3), etc. 
	[m1][trn][p] esp. BrE infml[/p] убираться, уходить:[/trn]
	\[IØ (usu. imper.)\] Buzz off, you children, I don't want you in my garden.[/ex]
	
cadge from
cadge off
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	→ cop from
	[m1][trn][p]BrE infml[/p] выпрашивать (что-л. у кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + from/off[/com]\] [ex]♦ Can I cadge a cigarette from you?[/ex]
	
cage in
cage up
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 сажать в клетку:[/trn]
	\[T1 + IN UP\] [ex]♦ You needn't worry about the tigers; they are safely caged in.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [b]be caged in/up[/b] чувствовать себя несвободным, связанным:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Mothers of young children often feel caged up.[/ex] 
	—caged-in/up [p]adj[/p]
	
cajole into
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]упрашивать, умасливать (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]X9 + into[/com]\] [ex]♦ Be nice to your father and see if you can cajole him into lending us the car tonight.[/ex]
	
cajole out of
	[p]v adv
	v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 отговаривать, часто с помощью лести и т. и.:[/trn]
	\[[com]Х9 + OUT + of[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'm sure the boy is not guilty; how can we cajole your grandfather out of punishing him?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 выманивать (деньги и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[[com]Х9 + OUT + of[/com]\] [ex]♦ Do you think, you can cajole some more money out of your father?[/ex]
	
cake in with
cake up with
	[p]v (adv) prep[/p]
	[m1][trn][b]be caked (in/up)[/b] with быть сплошь покрытым (чем-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ After the long ride, the horse was caked with mud.[/ex]
	
calculate for
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 рассчитывать на (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[L9 + for\] [ex]♦ We'd better calculate for a large crowd and provide plenty of food.[/ex] → calculate on (1)
	[m1][trn]2 esp. [p]AmE[/p] ожидать, думать, предполагать [i](обыч. о чём-л. неприятном).[/i][/trn]
	\[T1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ We didn't calculate for such bad weather.[/ex] 
	\[T4 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ We didn't calculate for having such bad weather.[/ex]
	\[V4a (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ We didn't calculate for the weather turning so bad.[/ex] → bargain for (2), reckon for
	
calculate on
calculate upon
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 быть готовым к (чему-л. ), рассчитывать на (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[L9 + on/upon\] We'd better calculate on a large crowd and provide plenty of food.[/ex] → calculate for (1)
	[m1][trn]2 esp. [p] AmE[/p] полагаться, рассчитывать (па что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'd like to come with you but that's not a promise, don't calculate on it.[/ex] 
	\[T4 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Don't calculate on going abroad this summer, we may not have enough money.[/ex]
	\[V4a (usu. simple tenses)) [ex]♦ Don't calculate on the weather being fine for your garden party.[/ex] → depend on (1), etc.
	
call about
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [p]BrE[/p] прийти к (кому-л.) по делу:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + about[/com]\] [ex]♦ There's a man at the door who says he's calling about your insurance.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [p]AmE[/p] звонить (кому-л.) по поводу (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + about[/com]\] [ex]♦ I tried to call about our meeting, but you weren't in.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 + about[/com]\] [ex]♦ I tried to call you about our meeting.[/ex]
	
call at
	[m0][p]v prep[/p] 
	→ call in2 (1), call into (1), touch at
	[m1][trn]заходить, заглядывать (куда-л.) ненадолго:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + at [/com]\] [ex]♦ I called at the office as I was passing, but you were out. The ship calls at several ports to pick up passengers before crossing the ocean.[/ex]
	
call away
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]отзывать, вызывать (куда-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'm sorry. Mr Sharp is not in the office at the moment, he's been called away to attend a meeting.[/ex]
	
call back
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 кричать в ответ, откликаться:[/trn]
	\[IØ + BACK\] [ex]♦ The man called from the other side of the river, and his friend called back.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 отзывать (представителя и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Our representative in Paris must be called back.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 вызывать в ответ, назад; возвращать:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ This medicine should call your strength back.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 снова посетить, снова заходить (куда-л.):[/trn]
	\[IØ (at)\] [ex]♦ The salesman will call back at any house he missed,[/ex] 
	—call-back [p]n, adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]5 перезвонить (кому-л.) по телефону:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Would you ask her to call me back?[/ex]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ I'll call back after dinner.[/ex]
	→ ring back
	[m1][trn]6 вспомнить, вызвать в памяти:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ I cannot call his face back.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 просить (кого-л.) вернуться на работу:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The director was called back to the office. Most of the strikers were called back to work after the strike was over,[/ex] 
	—call-back [p]n[/p]
	[m1][trn]8 брать (свои слова) назад:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ She wished she could call back her unfortunate remark.[/ex]
	
call by
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ bring over (1), etc.[p] 
	[m1][trn]not fml[/p] посетить проездом:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (often simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ When you're next in town, do call by.[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	→ go by2, (7), etc.
	[m1][trn]называть по имени:[/trn]
	\[X9 + by\] [ex]♦ If you call him just by his family name he won't answer you.[/ex]
	
call down
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]I кричать стоящим внизу:[/trn]
	\[IØ + DOWN\] [ex]♦ "Please come upstairs", she called down.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 позвать (кого-л.) сойти вниз:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + DOWN\] [ex]♦ Call Jane down again, she'll be late for work.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 [p]AmE not fml[/p] дать плохой отзыв о (чём-л.):[/trn]
	\[Tia\] [ex]♦ The newspapers called down Tom's latest book.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 [p]AmE infml[/p] ругать (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The director called Jim down for being late again. [/ex]
	→ tell off (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]5 [p]AmE[/p] вызывать (кого-л.) на бой, поединок:[/trn]
	\[T1 (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ A gentleman should call down any man who is rude to his wife.[/ex] call out (5)
	[m1][trn]6 [p]mil[/p] отдать приказ о начале бомбардировки (позиций противника):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ As a last effort, the general called down heavy bombing on the enemy positions.[/ex]
	
call down on
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[p]v prep[/p] 
	→ invoke on
	[m1][trn]призывать на головы (наказание и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[[com]Т1 (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The priests called down punishment on the people for their evil ways.[/ex]
	
call for
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 заходить (за чем-л. или кем-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + for[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'll call for you at 8 o'clock. The parcel was left at the post office to be called for.[/ex]
	→ pick up (2)
	[m1][trn]2 требовать, вызывать (что-л. или кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Henry called for the waiter in a loud voice. The opposition are calling for a general election.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 требовать, нуждаться (в чем-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ This trouble calls for quick action by the government. Your remark was not called for.[/ex] 
	—uncalled-for [p]adj[/p]
	
call forth
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ bring out (4), etc. 
	[p]fml[/p]
	[m1][trn]вызывать (к жизни), призывать к действию; мобилизовать (какие-л. качества):[/trn]
	\[[com]Т1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Trouble can call forth a person's best qualities.[/ex]
	
call forward
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]просить (кого-л.) выйти вперёд:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + FORWARD\] [ex]♦ All the prizewinners were called forward to receive their prizes.[/ex]
	
call in
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 заходить, навещать, останавливаться ненадолго:[/trn]
	\[L9 + IN (at) (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Please call in any time you're in town, I shall be glad to see you. The ship calls in at every port along this coast.[/ex] → call at, call into (1) 
	[m1][trn]2 звать (кого-л.) домой, в помещение:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + IN\] [ex]♦ Call the children in now, it's getting cold.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 приглашать (кого-л.) к себе домой:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + IN\] [ex]♦ She called all her friends in for a party.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 приглашать, вызывать (врача, специалиста и т. п.) для конеультации:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Mother was so ill last night that we had to call the doctor in.[/ex]
	→ bring in (5), come in (14), get in1 (8), have in1 (2)
	[m1][trn]5 потребовать возврата (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The makers have called in some cars with dangerous faults.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 требовать уплаты (долга):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The bank has threatened to call in all money lent more than twenty years ago.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 изымать из обращения (монеты или банкноты):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The government called in all old £J notes.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 [p]AmE[/p] сообщать по телефону на службу, в офис и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The director has just called in to say that she'll be late.[/ex]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ All the reporter's rushed to the telephone to call in their stories when the game was over.[/ex]
	→ phone in
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 зайти, заглянуть:[/trn]
	\[L9 + in\] [ex]♦ Will you call in the bank on your way home?[/ex] → call at, call into (1)
	[m1][trn]2 [b]call in question[/b] сомневаться, ставить под сомнение:[/trn]
	\[often pass.\] [ex]♦ The boy's honesty was never called in question,[/ex] —call into (5)
	
call into
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 зайти, заглянуть:[/trn]
	\[L9 + into\] Will you call into the bank on your way home?[/ex] → call at, call in2 (1), touch at
	[m1][trn]2 [b]call something into action[/b] призвать к действию:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ They called all possible forces into action to defeat the enemy.[/ex] → put out (23), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 [b]call something/somcone into being[/b] создать, вызвать к жизни:[/trn]
	\[[com] often pass. [/com]\] [ex]♦ The committee was called into being to discover the causes of the accident.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 [b]call something into play[/b] приводить в действие, пускать в ход:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Jim had to call all his skill into play to defeat his tennis opponent.[/ex] → be in (27b), bring into (8), come into (12)
	[m1][trn]5 [b]call into question[/b] сомневаться, ставить под сомнение:[/trn]
	\[often pass.\]. [ex]♦ The girl's honesty was never called into question.[/ex] —call in2 (2)
	
call off
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [p]AmE[/p] зачитывать список (имён и т. и ):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The teacher began to call off the names on his list.[/ex] → call over1 (2)
	[m1][trn]2 отменять (мероприятие):[/trn]
	\[T1\] (often simple tenses) [ex]♦ The cricket team had to call off the game because of rain.[/ex] → putt off1 (4), scrub out (3), scrub round (1)
	[m1][trn]3 отзывать, убирать, отогнать (кого-л. или собаку):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Call off your dog, he's biting my leg![/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 прекратить (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ It was decided to call off the search when there was no hope of finding the missing child alive.[/ex]
	
call on
call upon
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 нанести визит; посещать (по делу):[/trn]
	\[Т11 [ex]♦ Permit те to call on you next Tuesday afternoon. The salesman calls on the firm twice a month.[/ex] → wait on2 (3)
	[m1][trn]2 вызывать (кого-л.); призывать (кого-л.) сделать что-л.:[/trn]
	\[T1 (for) \] [ex]♦ I will now call on Jean (for an answer).[/ex] 
	\[V3 (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ I will now call on Mr Webster to drink the health of the happy pair. I feel called upon to reply to the speech. Many people call on God to help them.[/ex]
	
call out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 громко крикнуть, выкрикнуть:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Jane called out when she saw her friend across the street.[/ex]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Jane called out her friend's name when she saw her across the street.[/ex]
	→ cry out (1), roar out, shout out, thunder out, yell out
	[m1][trn]2 вызывать (кого-л.), делать вызов (кому-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1 (often pass.)\] [ex]♦ The doctor has been called out every night this week. All the firemen in the city were called out to fight the big fire.[/ex] → get out (18)
	[m1][trn]3 выявлять (что-л.), вызывать (что-л.) к жизни:[/trn]
	\[T1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Trouble can call out a person's best qualities.[/ex] → bring out (4), etc.
	[m1][trn]4 [p]not fml[/p] призывать (рабочих) к забастовке:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The worker's leader has called all the men out for more pay.[/ex] bring out (7), etc.
	[m1][trn]5 [p] BrE fml[/p] вызывать (кого-л.) на бой, на поединок:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ A gentleman should call out any man who is rude to his wife.[/ex] call down (5)
	
call over
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 попросить (кого-л.) подойти:[/trn]
	\[X9 + OVER\] [ex]♦ The man in the restaurant called the head waiter over to complain about the food.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 зачитывать список (имён, слов и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The teacher began to call over the names on his list.[/ex] 
	— call-over [p]n[/p] → call of (1)
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	→ haul over, rake over2 (2) 
	[m1][trn][b]call someone over the coals[/b] [p]not fml[/p] делать кому-л. выговор за проступок и т. п.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The director brought Jim into his office to call him over the coals for being late so often.[/ex]
	
call round
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn][p]not fml[/p] зайти, заглянуть (к кому-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + ROUND (at)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Why don't you call round one evening? I'm sorry I'm late, I called round at my sister's to see how she was.[/ex]
	
call to
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [b]call someone to account[/b] просить кого-л. дать объяснение:[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ The teacher has been called to account for the disorder in his classroom.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [b]call someone to arms[/b] призывать кого-л. на военную службу:[/trn]
	\[usu. pass \] [ex]♦ All the young men have been called to arms.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 [b]call attention to[/b] требовать (к себе) внимания:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Jane was too proud of her appearance, always calling attention to herself.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 [b]call a halt to[/b] останавливать (что-л.), положить конец (чему-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ We must call a halt to this senseless waste of life on the roads.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 [b]call something/someone to mind[/b] вспомнить что-л. или кого-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ I just can't call his face to mind at the moment.[/ex]
	→ bring to (19), come to2 (30), recall to (3)
	[m1][trn]6 [b]call to order[/b] призвать к порядку:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ The chairman called the committee to order and the meeting began.[/ex]
	
call together
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]собирать(ся), созывать(ся):[/trn]
	\[[com]X9 + TOGETHER (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Call all the members together and we'll take a vote. When I am unwell I find it difficult to call my thoughts together.[/ex]
	
call up
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 кричать снизу (кому-л. наверху):[/trn]
	\[IØ + UP\] [ex]♦ From the bottom of the stairs Jane called up "Have you seen my blue bag?"[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 esp. [p]BrE[/p] будить (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Please call me up at 7 o'clock tomorrow.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 esp. [p]AmE[/p] звонить (кому-л.) по телефону:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Please call me up tomorrow morning.[/ex]
	→ ring up (1)
	[m1][trn]4 esp. [p]BrE[/p] призвать (кого-л.) на военную службу в обязательном порядке; объявить (всеобщую мобилизацию):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ During the war, all younger men were called up to fight in the army, the navy, or the air force,[/ex] 
	—call-up [p]n, adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]5 напомнить; вспомнить:[/trn]
	\[T1 (usu. simple tenses) [ex]♦ The smell of those flowers calls up my childhood. Can you call up any of your thoughts on the subject?[/ex] → bring back (2), come back (4), come back to (2), summon up (2)
	[m1][trn]6 вызывать (кого-л. или что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The strange old lady claims to be able to call up spirits from the dead.[/ex]
	→ raise from (2), rise from (4), summon up (4)
	
calm down
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 успокаивать (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1 +DOWN] [ex]♦ Try to calm the children down, will you, they're too excited.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 успокоиться; стихнуть:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Calm down, there's nothing to worry about. The wind seems to be calming down at last.[/ex]
	
camp out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 жить в палатках (обыкн. на отдыхе):[/trn]
	\[IØ + OUT\] [ex]♦ The children love camping out when we go on holiday.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 временно жить (у кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (with)[/com]\] [ex]♦ When they were first married, Jim and Mary had no home of their own, so they had to camp out with Mary's parents.[/ex]
	
camp up
	[p]v adv[/p] 
	→ ham up
	[m1][trn][b]camp it up[/b] [p]infml[/p] переборщить, стараясь рассмешить:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ You might offend your hosts if you camp it up at the party in your usual way.[/ex]
	
campaign against
	[m0][p]v prep[/p] 
	→ campaign for (2), etc.
