catch on
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [p]not fml[/p] становиться известным, модным и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ I don't think this strange new fashion will catch on.[/ex] → take on1 (9)
	[m1][trn]2 [p]not fml[/p] понять сказанное (особ. шутку):[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Would you mind repeating that, I didn't quite catch on.[/ex] → latch on (1)
	[m1][trn]3 [p]not fml[/p] быстро схватывать, понимать; быть понятливым:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (to) (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ An enquiring mind quickly catches on to a new idea. The best thing about this new worker is that he catches on very fast.[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 зацепиться случайно за (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + on (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ My coat caught on a nail and tore.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 + on[/com]\] [ex]♦ I would have been early enough, but I caught my coat on a nail just as I was leaving.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [b]catch someone on the hop[/b] [p]infml[/p] застать кого-л. за каким-л. неожиданным занятием, в неподходящий момент и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ I went in without knocking and there was his secretary sitting on his knee; I'd caught him on the hop.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 [b]catch someone on the raw[/b] [p]not fml[/p] задевать кого-л. за живое:[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ You'll have to be more careful what you say; you really caught him on the raw when you mentioned his first wife.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 [b]catch someone on the wrong foot[/b] [p]not fml[/p] застать кого-л. в неподходящий момент и т. п.:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Joe came to the party too early and caught us on the wrong foot.[/ex]
	
catch out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 не застать (кого-л.) дома или на работе и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + OUT (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ He was here all morning, but you've just caught him out.[/ex] → catch in (1)
	[m1][trn]2 [i](в крикете и других играх с мячом)[/i] удалять (игрока), который при подаче поймал мяч прямо битой:[/trn]
	\[T1 + OUT (often pass.)\] [ex]♦ Three of the team were caught out just before tea.[/ex] → be out (7), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 [p]not fml[/p] хитростью поймать кого-л. на лжи и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'm sure the prisoner is not telling the truth: talk to him and see if you can catch him out. The examiners will try to catch you out, so have all your answers well prepared.[/ex]
	
catch red-handed
	[p]♦ v adj[/p]
	→ catch in2 (2)
	[m1][trn][p]not fml[/p] поймать (кого-л.) с поличным, поймать (кого-л.) на месте преступления:[/trn]
	\[T1b (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The policeman followed the thieves until they actually picked up the jewellery, when he caught them red-handed.[/ex]
	
catch short
	[p]v adj[/p] 
	→ take short
	[m1][trn][b]be caught short[/b] не иметь запаса чего-л.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Have our meat delivered to your freezer, so that you're never caught short when unexpected guests arrive.[/ex]
	
catch up
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 быстро схватить (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[X9 + UP\] [ex]♦ The thieves caught up the money and ran.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 подвешивать (что-л.), закреплять (что-л.) вверху:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + UP (usu. pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ My coat was caught up on the hook. The big red curtain was caught up with a golden rope.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 задерживать (кого-л.) на время, удерживать (кого-л.) на месте:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I was caught up in the busy traffic again this evening.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 догнать (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I had to run to catch her up.[/ex]
	\[IØ (with)\] [ex]♦ I had to run to catch up with her.[/ex]
	→ come up with (1), get up with (2), keep up (7), keep up with (1)
	[m1][trn]5 идти вровень с (кем-л. или чем-л.):[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ We shall have to work hard to catch the other firm up.[/ex]
	\[IØ (on, with)\] [ex]♦ He spent six months catching up with his studies. When you've been away, it takes a long time to catch up on the local news. The tax people will catch up with you in the end. [/ex]→ come up with (2), keep up (8), keep up with (2)
	[m1][trn]6 закрепить (что-л.) несколькими стежками, напр., чтобы сделать платье короче:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Catch up the lower edge with light sewing so that it doesn't show.[/ex] catch in (2)
	[m1][trn]7 [p]AmE not fml[/p] показывать, что (кто-л.) неправ; находить просчёты (у кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ The examiners caught him up on the third question. Several people in the crowd caught the politician up during his speech.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 [b]be caught up in[/b] глубоко интересоваться (чем-л.), серьёзно заниматься (чем-л.):[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Both my children are caught up in the present fashion for rock, music. Most citizens do not get caught up in local events. Many politicians started by being caught up in the party when they were very young.[/ex]
	
