	[m1][trn]2 случаться с (кем-л.); охватить, овладеть:[/trn]
	\[T1 (no pass.)\] [ex]♦ An attack of faintness came over him. What has come over you?[/ex] → come on2 (3)
	[m1][trn]3 [p]infml[/p] разыгрывать из себя (кого-л.), чтобы произвести впечатление на (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[Dl\] [ex]♦ He's been coming the great political leader over us all since he won the election.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 come it over [p]infml[/p] важничать, держаться высокомерно:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Don't try to come it over me![/ex]
	
come short to
	[p]v adj prep[/p]
	→ fall short of 
	[m1][trn]не хватать, не соответствовать (ожиданиям):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The supply has come short of our needs. Your work comes short of the expected standard.[/ex]
	
come through
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 проникать сквозь, проходить насквозь:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + THROUGH (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Just then the branches divided and a small head came through. The leather has worn, and the point of the knife comes through.[/ex]
	→ get through1 (1), go through1 (1)
	[m1][trn]2 прибывать по расписанию, прибывать как ожидалось:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Has the train come through? My examination results will come through by post. Your permission to take a week's holiday with pay has just come through.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 появляться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The sun came through after days of rain.[/ex]
	→ break through1 (1)
	[m1][trn]4 быть видным, проявляться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ His ability did not come through when we examined his papers.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]→ show through1 (2) 
	[m1][trn]5 [i](о крикетном мяче)[/i] подлетать на небольшую высоту:[/trn]
	\[L9 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The ball came through at a surprisingly low height.[/ex] → get up1 (13)
	[m1][trn]6 передаваться (по радио и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[IØ (on)\] [ex]♦ The message has just come through on the radio that the general is dying.[/ex]
	→ be through1 (2), get through1 (6), get through to1 (1), put through1 (2)
	[m1][trn]7 остаться в живых, уцелеть:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (usu. simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ Most of our men came through unharmed.[/ex]
	→ bring through1, carry through1 (3), pull round1 (2), pull through1 (2)
	[m1][trn]8 выпутаться из неприятного положения:[/trn]
	\[IØ (with)\] [ex]♦ I knew that in the end John would come through (with the answer to our difficulty). Whatever the trouble, he always comes through.[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 проникать сквозь, просачиваться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + through (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Just then the branches divided and a small head came through the leaves. The rain is coming through the roof.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 пронизывать:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ His opinion came through all that he was saying. A feeling of sadness comes through his music.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 пройти через (что-л.), перенести (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Bill came through his operation as cheerful as ever. All my family came through the war.[/ex]
	→ bring through2, carry through2 (2), pull through2 (3)
	
come through with
come across with
come down with
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	→ come down (10)[p] 
	[m1][trn]not fml[/p] обеспечивать (обыкн. деньгами или информацией):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Good old Arthur came through with a cheque to cover the last payment.[/ex]
	
come to
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 приходить в себя:[/trn] 
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The girl fainted, but she came to when we threw drops of water on her face.[/ex]
	→ bring to1 (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 приходить снова в хорошее настроение:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Father was in a very bad temper this morning, but he had come to by dinner time.[/ex]
	→ come around (7)
	[m1][trn]3 [i](о судне и т. п.)[/i] приводить(ся) к ветру:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Take the wheel, see if you can make her come to.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 [i]♦(о судне и т. п.)[/i] прекращать движение:[/trn]
	\[IØ (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ As the wind dropped, the boat came to.[/ex] → bring to1 (2), bring up (6), heave to
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 приезжать, прибывать, подходить (к ко- му-л. или чему-л.); посещать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + to[/com]\] [ex]♦ When did you come to London? All the children came to the teacher as soon as she called them. Please come to the concert with me on Saturday. Several trees came to the ground in last night's storm.[/ex]
	→ go to (1), take to (1)
	[m1][trn]2 настигать (кого-л.), происходить с (кем-л.), доходить до (чего-л.):[/trn] 
	\[[com]IØ + to[/com]\] [ex]♦ Fame came to him while he was still young. I don't want any harm to come to the children. At this point, the water only comes to your knees.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 превращаться (во что-л.), доходить до (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ What is the world coming to? It is to be hoped that the quarrel between the two nations will not come to war.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 достигать (результата, условия, времени), составлять (сумму):[/trn]
	\[T1 (no pass., simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The bill comes to £5.50. When the milk comes to the boil, add the powder. This happy state of affairs must not be allowed to come to an end. The two brothers quicly came to blows during their quarrel. Those criminals will come to justice in the end. Will the children come to any harm there? The committee have not yet come to a decision. When it comes to 5 o'clock, I must turn the meat over. We should be able to come to a special arrangement with our bank.[/ex] → get into (11), etc.
	[m1][trn]5 подходить (к проблеме), приступать (к работе):[/trn] 
	\[[com]Х9 + with[/com]\] [ex]♦ It's lucky that you came to your marriage with an open mind. It's no use coming to this kind of work with already formed ideas,[/ex]
	→ bring to2 (2)
	[m1][trn]6 вспомниться (кому-л.), всплыть в памяти (у кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] It + T1 (no pass., simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Suddenly it came to me where I had seen her before.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ At last the name of the book came to mind.[/ex]
	→ come on2 (5)
	[m1][trn]7 [i](о деньгах, собственности и т. п.)[/i][/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The house came to me after my father's death. You will have some money coming to you next month.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 [p]infml[/ex] [i](о наказании и т. п.)[/i] причитаться (кому-л.), следовать (кому-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., continuous tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ You've got it coming to you. I hope he gets what's coming to him.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 кончиться (чем-л.), привести (к чему-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'm disappointed that my efforts have come to so little. All his plans came to nothing.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 достичь чего-л. в жизни:[/trn]
	\[T1 (no pass.)\] [ex]♦ The boy has no character, he will never come to much.[/ex]
	→ amout to (3)
	[m1][trn]11 опуститься до (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Has he come to this?[/ex]
	\[[com]T4 (no pass., usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ He came to selling matches.[/ex]
	→ come down to (6), reduce to (12)
	[m1][trn]12 касаться (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ When it comes to politics, I know nothing.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]13 означать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ His speech comes to this; the country is deeply in debt.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]14составлять (сумму):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The bill came to £19.[/ex]
	→ add up to (1), amount to (1)
	[m1][trn]15 come to someone's aid/assistance/help прийти к кому-л. на помощь:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ All the villagers came to our assistance in searching for the missing child.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]16 come to attention:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]a [p]mil[/p] стать в положение «смирно»:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ All the soldiers came to attention as the general arrived.[/ex]
	→ stand at (3), etc.: 
	[m1][trn]b also come to (someone's) knowledge/notice прийти в чью-л. голову, привлечь чьё-л. внимание:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Why hasn't this come to my attention sooner? It has come to my attention that you have been cheating.[/ex] → bring to2 (4b)
	[m1][trn]17 come to the boil [p]not fml[/p] дойти до критической точки:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The university's money troubles have come to the boil because of the recent government cuts.[/ex] → bring to2 (5)
	[m1][trn]18 if/when it comes to the crunch/push [p]not fml[/p] если/когда дела дойдут до крайности, если/когда наступит рещающий момент:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ If it should come to the crunch, we can always sell the house to get some money.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]19 come to a dead end зайти в тупик:[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Our enquiry came to a dead end when we learned that the woman had left the country.[/ex]
	→ be at (8), bring to2 (7)
	[m1][trn]20 come to one's ears услышать или узнать:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ It came to my ears that he had lost his job.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]21 come to one's feet вставать в знак уважения:[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ When I was at school, all the pupils had to come to their feet whenever a teacher entered the classroom. The whole crowd came to their feet to cheer the young singer.[/ex]
	→ get to (11), etc.
	[m1][trn]22 come to the fore стать заметным, значительным, влиятельным:[/trn]
	\[no pass., often simple tenses\] A worker with ability and character will always come to the fore. [/ex]→ bring to2 (10)
	[m1][trn]23 come to grief [p]not fml[/p] попасть в беду, потерпеть неудачу:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ When money became scarce, the firm came to grief. I'd rather you didn't have a motorcycle, you might come to grief on the road.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]24 come to grips with схватиться (с чем-л.), бороться (с чем-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I have to come to grips with this difficult question.[/ex] → bring to2 (11). get to (13)
	[m1][trn]25 come to hand оказаться поблизости, под рукой:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Your letter of the 16th has come to hand, and I am passing your request to the chairman. I would use a pencil if one came to hand readily.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]26 come to a head достигнуть решающей стадии:[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦Matters have come to a head in the peace talks; tomorrow they will either succeed or cease.[/ex] → bring to2 (13) 
	[m1][trn]27 come to heel [p]not fml[/p] подчиняться:[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦All those lazy workers must come to heel, or they will lose their jobs. [/ex]→ bring to2 (14)
	[m1][trn]28 come to life стать интересным, оживиться:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The dull play really came to life in the performance by these young people.[/ex]
	→ bring to2 (17)
	[m1][trn]29 come to light обнаружиться:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The family were so pleased when the long-lost jewels came to light.[/ex] → bring to2 (18)
	[m1][trn]30 come to mind вспомниться:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Although I know it well, the name just won't come to mind.[/ex] → bring to2 (19), call to (5), come on2 (5), recall to (3)
	[m1][trn]31 come to naugh/nought свестись к нулю, пропасть, оказаться бесполезным:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ All his efforts came to naught since he died before completing the job.[/ex]
	→ bring to2 (20)
	[m1][trn]32 come to oneself прийти в себя, очнуться:[/trn] 
	\[usu. simple tenses\] [ex]♦She came to herself when we threw cold water on her face. I had been lost in thought for so long that it was only when the train whistle blew that I came to myself.[/ex] → bring to2 (15)
	[m1][trn]33 come to pass [p]fml[/p] произойти, случиться:[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ I doubt if the government's promises will ever come to pass. [/ex]→ bring to2 (21)
	[m1][trn]34 come to such a pass/a pretty pass [p]not fml[/p] разрушиться; прийти в скверное состояние [i](о делах, ситуации и т. п.)[/i]:[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ I know you two have never agreed, but how did your relationship come to such a pass? It's come to a pretty pass when people can earn more from unemployment pay than from work.[/ex] → bring to2 (22)
	[m1][trn]35 come to the point:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а говорить по существу дела:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Please come to the point, I want to know what you're trying to say.[/ex] 
	[m1][trn]b достичь определённой точки, стадии:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ It has come to the point where I must speak to the boy severely. I hope it never comes to the point that we have to dismiss workers.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]36 come to someone's/the rescue спасать ко- го-л. или что-л.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ If that woman keeps me talking, will you please come to my rescue and take me away from her on some urgent business? The government may have to come to the rescue of the mine in order to save the workers jobs.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]37 come to rest останавливаться:[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ The car at last came to rest in a field after the driver had struggled with the controls.[/ex] → bring to2 (23)
	[m1][trn]38 come to the same thing быть равным или таким же, совпадать:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ "It all comes to the same thing at the end."[/ex] (Robert Browning, [ex]♦ Any Wife to Any Husband)[/ex]
	[m1][trn]	39 come to one's senses:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а прийти в себя:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ She came to her senses when we threw cold water on her face,[/ex] 
	[m1][trn]b взяться за ум:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ After being in love with that worthless boy for so long, she at last came to her senses and knew he would never be a good husband.[/ex] → bring to2 (24)
	[m1][trn]40 come to something [p]not fml[/p] дойти до крайнего предела, шокировать своей сущностью:[/trn]
	\[[com] often perfect or continuous tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ It's coming to something when workers can earn more from unemployment pay than from their jobs.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]41 come to terms прийти к соглашению (с кем-л.):[/trn]
	\[no pass.\] [ex]♦ The two fighting nations must come to terms (with each other).[/ex]
	[m1][trn]42 come to terms with мириться, примиряться (с лишениями, трудностями и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[no pass.\] [ex]♦ After years in a hospital bed, you come to terms with your illness.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]43 if it comes to that если необходимо:[/trn]
	\[[com] no pass.[/com]\] [ex]♦ let me drive you home, or if it comes to that, you could spend the night here.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]44 come to this достичь какого-л. уровня, условия, обыкн. отрицательного: 
	[ex]♦ Have we really come to this, that we can't speak without quarrelling? So this is what the city has come to since the developers bought all the land![/ex]
	[m1][trn]45 if the worst comes to the worst если случится худшее, если обстоятельства станут крайними:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ If the worst comes to the worst, we can always sell the house and live on that money.[/ex]
	
come together
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 собраться; встретиться:[/trn]
	\[L9 +TOGETHER\] [ex]♦ The family must come together for the parents silver wedding. Jim and Mary first came together during the war.[/ex] → bring together (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 [p]infml[/p] одновременно испытать оргазм, кончить вместе:[/trn]
	\[L9 + TOGETHER\] [ex]♦ The lovers came together.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 уладить разногласия:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ I'm so glad that you two have decided to come together at last. The different parties in the peace talks came together last week to give a statement.[/ex]
	→ bring together (3)
	
come true
