	[m1][trn]1 падать назад на (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + BACK + on (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Luckily she fell back on grass or she might have hurt her head. The tired traveller fell back on the bed and at once fell asleep.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [p]mil[/p] отступать, отходить до (какого-л. места):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The battle is going badly for us, we'd better fall back on the town in the hills.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 прибегать к (чему-л. ); обратиться к (ко- му-л.) за помощью, обыкн. когда другие меры исчерпаны:[/trn]
	\[T1 (pass. rare, usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Doctors sometimes fall back on old cures when modern medicine does not work. If we spend all our holiday money, we can fall back on the traveller's cheques. The band were so short of musicians that they had to fall back on students. I'm glad we kept some money in the bank so that we always have something to fall back on.[/ex] → drive back on
	
fall behind
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 отставать; терять темп; передвинуться на позицию ниже или сзади остальных:[/trn] 
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ We thought the horse would win, but he fell behind halfway through the race. Don't fall behind now, just when you're doing so well- Production has fallen behind in the last few months.[/ex] → lag behind (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 запоздать, напр., с платежами:[/trn]
	\[IØ with)\] [ex]♦ If your payments of rent fall behind, you will be asked to leave. If you fall behind with the rent, you will be asked to leave. [/ex]→ get behind (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 становиться хуже; терять качество:[/trn] 
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The work of this class has been falling behind recently.[/ex] → drop away (3), drop off (5), fall away (4), fall off1 (4), go off1 (7)
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 упасть, завалиться за (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + behind (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The photograph has fallen behind the piano, and I can't get it out.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 западать, закатиться за (что-л.):[/trn]
