	[m1][trn]15 сосчитать; найти (сумму):[/trn]
	\[T1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ These days, scientists use computers to help them to get out the difficult calculations concerned with space travel. [/ex]→ come out (10), work out (4)
	[m1][trn]16 [i](в крикете)[/i] удалять, отпускать (игрока, который бьёт битой):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ If we don't get the next two players out before tea, there won't be time to finish the game, and so we can't win. How was Botham got out? Oh, he was caught out at last after a very fine performance.[/ex]
	\[IØ (simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ How did Botham get out?[/ex] → be out (7), etc.
	[m1][trn]17 заставлять прекратить работу, призывать к забастовке:[/trn]
	\[T1b (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ If we get all the workers out, we may be able to force the government to act.[/ex] → bring out (7), etc.
	[m1][trn]18 поднять на ноги (полицию, врача и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[T1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ We've got police out all over the area, looking for the missing girl. Sorry to get you out at this late hour, doctor, but Mother became suddenly ill in the middle of the night.[/ex] → call out (2) 
	[m1][trn]19 get one's rag out [p]infml[/p] рассердиться:[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ There's no need to get your rag out just because of one unfortunate remark.[/ex]
	
get out of
	[p]v adv
	v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 вылезать, выходить, выбираться; выводить (откуда-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + OUT + of[/com]\] [ex]♦ The climber couldn't get out of the deep hole in the snow until his companions brought more rope. The door locked accidentally, and I couldn't get out of the bathroom.[/ex]
	\[X9 + OUT + of (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Get the horses out of the burning building first before you attend to the fire. [/ex]→ alight from (1), etc., be out of (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 вынимать, вытаскивать (что-л. откуда-л.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + OUT + of (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Can you get this nail out of the wall? I can't. [/ex]→ take out (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 выходить, высаживать(ся) из поезда, машины ит. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + OUT + of[/com]\] [ex]♦ Two men with guns got out of the car and threatened the shopkeeper.[/ex]
	\[[com]X9 + OUT + of (usu. simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ Why can't they get the cases and bags out of the plane faster?[/ex]
	→ alight from (1), get out (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]4 брать (деньги из банка, книгу в библиотеке и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + OUT + of (usu. simple tenses) \] [ex]♦ You can only get six books out of the library at any one time. I shall have to get some more money out of the bank.[/ex] → draw out (11), get out (6), take out of (4)
	[m1][trn]5 списывать, переписывать (что-л. откуда-л.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + OUT + of (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ His story isnt original, he got it out of a book. [/ex]→ obtain from, etc.
	[m1][trn]6 удалять(ся); уходить, убегать; убирать, забирать:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (pass. rare)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I got out of the meeting as soon as I could, but it went on very late. After raising a family, many women feel the need to get out of the house and be recognized for their own abilities. Once you're in it, you can't easily get out of the race for trade. Why don't you get out of the country if you don't like it? We'll give you till midday to get out of town. Get out of my garden! Get out of my sight![/ex]
	\[D1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Get those dirty boots out of my kitchen. I shall get the children out of that school if what they say about their teachers is true.[/ex] → push off (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]7 бежать, совершать побег; вытекать, просачиваться; выпускать:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Two prisoners got out of prison yesterday. The rabbits have got out of their cages again, and are eating all the vegetables in the garden. Gas has been getting out of the main pipe, which burst in the severe cold.[/ex]
	→ get out (8), worm out of (1), wriggle out of (1)
	[m1][trn]8 разузнать (что-л. от кого-л.); добиться сведений:[/trn]
	\[[com]D1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'll get the truth out of the prisoner if it takes all night. When shall we get a statement out of the government? Not a word could be got out of the child.[/ex]
	→ get out (10), worm out of (2) 
	[m1][trn]9 получать, извлекать (что-л. из чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]D1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ You get out of any performance only as much as you put in. What does he get out of life, for all his money? You won't get much income out of your book for the first year. The children are always trying to get more money out of their father.[/ex] \[V4b (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ I get such a lot out of watching my grandson play.[/ex]
	→ get out (11) 
	[m1][trn]10 отвыкнуть от (чего-л.); отучить от (привычки):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ How can I get out of the habit of smoking?[/ex]
	\[D1) [ex]♦ Joining a group will help to get you out of the habit. [/ex]
	→ get into (8)
	[m1][trn]11 выпутаться из (беды); выбраться из (затруднительного положения); избавляться от (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Trouble with the director is not easy to get out of. I don't suppose I shall ever be able to get out of debt.[/ex]
	\[[com]D1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ A word from the teacher in your favour could get you out of trouble. You got yourself into this: now get yourself out of it.[/ex]
	→ get into (II), etc.
	[m1][trn]12 оставлять (в беспорядке и т. п); забывать; прекращать находиться в прежнем положении:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ How did my papers get out of order? I had them all carefully arranged. You can soon get out of practice unless you play the piano regularly. No singer should ever get out of breath.[/ex]
	\[D1 (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Who's been getting my papers out of order? I had them all carefully arranged.[/ex] → be in (3), get into (12) 
	[m1][trn]13 избегать (ответственности и т. п ), уклоняться от (ответственности, исполнения долга):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ You can't get out of your share of the blame so easily. Why should you get out of the heavy work just because you're a girl? There are always some unpleasant jobs that can't be got out of.[/ex] (1'4 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Well, you get out of doing the dishes just because you're a boy![/ex] \[D1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Crying won't get you out of your punishment.[/ex] → get off (5), let off2 (2), shuffle out of (3), slide out of (2), slip out of (4), sneak out of (2), squirm out of, worm out of (3), wriggle out of (2)
	[m1][trn]14 get out of bed on the wrong side [p]infml[/p] быть (с утра) в плохом настроении; встать с левой ноги:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ \often simple[/ex] (crises) [ex]♦ What's the matter with you today? Did you get out of bed on the wrong side? You've been doing nothing but complain.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]15 get the best/most out of использовать в полной мерс; достичь наивысшего результата:[/trn] 
	\[usu. simple tenses\] [ex]♦ You will get the best out of your washing machine by never filling it too full. You get the most out of students by respecting their views.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]16 get blood out of a stone [p]infml[/p] с большим трудом выбить (деньги и т. п ):[/trn] 
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Getting a pay rise from the director is like trying to get blood out of a stone.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]17 get change out of [p]infml[/p] получать удовлетворительный ответ или результат:[/trn] 
	\[simple tenses, + any, much, no, some\] [ex]♦ If he tries to make me join the Party, he'll get no change out of me! Did you get much change out of the director? No, he never gives a straight answer, so I don't know what his opinion is.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]18 get a charge/kick out of [p]infml[/p] получать удовольствие, быть в восторге:[/trn] 
	\[usu. simple tenses\] [ex]♦ "I get a kick out of you."[/ex] (song) [ex]♦ Jim gets a kick out of beating his director at tennis. I can't see what kicks he gets out of such loud music.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]19 get out of date становиться немодным, выходить из моды:[/trn]
	\[[ex]♦ usu. simple tenses\ The trouble with interesting clothes is that they get out of date so quickly.[/ex]
	→ be up to (7), etc.
	[m1][trn]20 get out of one's depth:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а попасть на глубокое место, не доставать дна:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I'm not а strong swimmer, so I shall be careful not to get out of my depth,[/ex] 
	[m1][trn]b [p]not fml[/p] пытаться сделать что-л. слишком трудное:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The pianist began well, but when he reached the difficult parts of the piece, he got out of his depth and made a lot of mistakes.[/ex]
	→ be out of (8), go out of (6)
	[m1][trn]21 get out of earshot уйти слишком далеко, чтобы услышать; быть вне пределов слышимости:[/trn] 
	\[usu. simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Don't let the baby get out of earshot, I want to hear what he's doing.[/ex] → come within (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]22 get out of focus быть не в фокусе, расплывчатым, неясным:[/trn] 
	\[usu. simple tenses\] [ex]♦ The film got out of focus when the camera broke. I think you've got the picture out of focus, I can't see the faces clearly, fl ecause of her great love for him, she got his character out of focus.[/ex]
	→ go out of (9)
	[m1][trn]23 get out of the groovc/rut [p]infml[/p] уйти c проторённой дорожки, выбраться из рутины:[/trn]
	\[usu. simple tenses\] [ex]♦I had to get out of the rut, so I changed my job.[/ex] → get into (25) 
	[m1][trn]24 get out of hand отбиться от рук, выйти из повиновения; выйти из-под контроля:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The difficult class got out of hand when an inexperienced teacher was put in charge.[/ex] → be out of (10)
	[m1][trn]25 get out of the hang of doing something [p]not fml[/p] забывать, терять навык; утратить сноровку, привычку:[/trn]
