get over
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 переходить через дорогу, переправлять(ся) через реку, перелезать через стену:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + OVER (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ How can we get over? The traffic's so busy.[/ex]
	\[X9 + OVER (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The new boats get people over to the island in half the time. Give me a push up, will you, I can't get my leg over.[/ex] → come over1 (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 приходить, приезжать куда-л., навещать кого-л.:[/trn]
	\[L9 + OVER\] [ex]♦ When the weather's better, do get over to see us. Get over here as fast as you can, there's been an accident. [/ex]→ bring over (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 довести до сознания:[/trn]
	\[T1 (to) (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ It takes an experienced politician to get such an unpopular message over.[/ex] 
	\[IØ (to) (simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Did your speech get over (to the crowd)?[/ex] → come across1 (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]4 перенести, отделаться:[/trn]
	\[T1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ You'll be glad to get your operation over.[/ex] → be over1 (2), get through1 (8), have over1 (2)
	[m1][trn]5 get something over (and done) with [p]not fml[/p] покончить с (чем-л. неприятным):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ You'll be able to enjoy your holiday now that you've got the examinations over and done with.[/ex] → finish with (4), etc.
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 переходить, переправлять(ся), перелезать через (что-л.), пересекать (дорогу, реку и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + over[/com]\] [ex]♦ The smallest boy was unable to get over the wall as fast as the others. You can get over the road more safely at the traffic lights.[/ex]
	\[[com]X9 + over (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ How do you get a horse over a high fence? The teacher got the children safely over the busy road.[/ex]
	→ go across2, etc.
	[m1][trn]2 проходить (расстояние); перевозить (на какое-л. расстояние):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + over (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ These new cars get over the miles so smoothly. All the horses got over the course, but two of them were badly hurt.[/ex]
	\[[com]X9 + over (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ A lighter bicycle will get you over the distance so much more easily.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 справляться с (чувством, трудностями и т. п.); преодолевать:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The committee will have to find means to get over the difficulty. I see no way of getting over the lack of paper. The singer had to learn to get over her fear of the public.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 поправляться, приходить в себя; вновь обретать успех и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ It always takes some time to get over the shock of someone's death. I hope you soon get over your troubles. A shock like that isn't easily got over.[/ex]
	→ recover from (2), recuperate from
	[m1][trn]5 перестать страдать по кому-л.; забыть кого-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Jim would never get over Mary if they separated. Has Alice got over her former boyfriend yet?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 [p]not fml[/p] переносить; свыкаться с мыслью; приходить в себя (после шока):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass, usu. neg., simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ I can't get over your news, I would never have thought it possible! Tell me again, I can't get over it! We can't get over his sudden decision to leave the country![/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 опровергать, отрицать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. neg., simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Even their lawyer can't get over the fact that they knew they were stealing.[/ex]
	
get past
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 проходить, проезжать мимо:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + PAST (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The crowd moved aside to let the firemen get past.[/ex]
	→ come along (1), come by1 (1), fly by1 (1), get by1 (1), go by1 (1), go past1 (1)
	[m1][trn]2 быть приемлемым, сходить, годиться:[/trn] 
	\[IØ (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ This dress is rather old but I'd like to wear it to visit these people: do you think it will get past?[/ex] get by1 (4), get by with (4).
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 проходить мимо (кого-л. или чего-л.); миновать (кого-л. или что-л.); проводить мимо:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + past (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ She couldn't get past all the people, and so lost him in the crowd. I don't think that the ship will get past the enemy guns. Can you get past the open door without being seen?[/ex]
	\[X9 + past (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The horse that gets its nose past the winning post first is the winner. How did the captain get the ship past the enemy guns?[/ex] go past5 (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 проходить, наступать (о времени):[/trn] 
	\[L9 + past\] [ex]♦ If it gets past midnight and they haven't returned, I shall telephone the police.[/ex] → be past (2)
	[m1][trn]3 продвигать(ся) дальше, чем; достигать (какого-л. уровня):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Hasn't this class got past lesson three?[/ex]
	\[01\] [ex]♦ Even the best teacher cannot get an unmusical student past simple piano exercises.[/ex] → get beyond (2), go beyond (4), go past2 (2)
	[m1][trn]4 проходить мимо, миновать, не встречая препятствий; быть принятым:[/trn]
	\[T1 (no pass., usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ How did the escaping prisoners get past the guards? What makes you think that such careless work will get past me? Your suggestion has got past the first stage and will now be examined by the committee.[/ex] → get by2 (2)
	[m1][trn]5 быть слишком трудным для (кого-л.); сталкиваться с трудностями в работе:[/trn]
	\[[com]Т1 (no pass., often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I enjoyed the book as far as Part Two: after that, it got a bit past me. It gets past me how he does it! The children tried to build a hut in the garden, but the work got past them and they had to ask their mother to help.[/ex]
	\[T4\] [ex]♦ Jim's father felt that he had got past running the business on his own, and advertised for help.[/ex] → be beyond (4), be past (3), get beyond (3), go beyond (5), go past2 (3)
	[m1][trn]6 get past caring перестать заботиться (о чём-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ I've got past caring how to pay the bills; people will just have to wait, that's all.[/ex]
	→ go past2 (4), etc.
	[m1][trn]7 get past endurance становиться невыносимым, несносным:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Your rudeness has got past endurance — kindly leave my house![/ex]
	→ get beyond (5), etc.
	[m1][trn]8 get past it становиться слишком старым:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ This firm keeps an eye on its older workers, and dismisses them the minute they get past it. I'm sorry to tell you that your piano is getting past it; the wood has dried out, loosening the strings, so it will never be in tune again.[/ex]
	→ be past (6)
	[m1][trn]9 get past a joke переставать быть смешным; становиться слишком серьёзным:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Your continual lateness has got past a joke; be on time tomorrow, or you will be dismissed.[/ex]
	→ be beyond (7), get beyond (6), go beyond (8), go past2 (6).
	
get right
	[p]v adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 не делать, не совершать ошибок (при подсчёте и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + right\] [ex]♦ Start that scene again and get it right this time! If I do the sum three times I'm more likely to get it right.[/ex]
	→ get wrong (1)
	[m1][trn]2 быть правильно информированным о (чём-л.), правильно понимать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + right\] [ex]♦ Did I get that right — did you actually say that our best worker should be dismissed? Newspaper reports rarely get anyone's name right.[/ex]
	→ get wrong (2).
	
get round
get around
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 ездить, путешествовать:[/trn]
	\[L9 + ROUND\] [ex]♦ It's easy in this city to get round by bus. [/ex]→ get about1 (1), go about1 (1), take about1 (1), take around1 (1)
	[m1][trn]2 вновь ходить, двигаться (после болезни):[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Mother is much better now, thank you, she's able to get round a bit more.[/ex]
	→ get about1 (2), go about1 (2)
	[m1][trn]3 [i](о новостях и т. п.)[/i] распространяться:[/trn]
	\[IØ (often continuous tenses)\] [ex]♦ Stories have been getting round concerning the government's secret intentions.[/ex] → go about1 (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]4 навещать, посещать (кого-л.); приводить кого-л. к кому-л.:[/trn]
	\[IØ (to) (usu. simple tenses) [ex]♦ When are you going to get round to our house?[/ex]
	\[T1 (to) (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Do get your new boyfriend round to see us. [/ex]→ bring over (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]5 убедить, уговорить (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1 (to) (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ We'll soon get him round (to our point of view).[/ex] → bring around (2), bring over (3), bring round (3), come around (6), come over1(5), fetch over (2), fetch round (3), go over to (5), pull round (4)
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 ездить, путешествовать (где-л.); доставлять, возить (куда-л):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + round[/com]\] [ex]♦ The quickest way to get round the city is by underground train. How many horses got round the course?[/ex]
	\[X9 + round (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ A taxi will get you round the city faster.[/ex]
	→ get about2, etc.
	[m1][trn]2 обходить (препятствие, закон и т. п.); обходиться без кого-л.:[/trn]
	\[T1 (often simple tenses) \] [ex]♦If you are clever, you can sometimes get round the tax laws. Don't try to get round the question by changing the subject. We could get round the lack of players by removing the last piece of music from the concert programme.[/ex] → scrub round (2), skate round (3), slide over (2), slide round (2)
	[m1][trn]3 заставлять, убеждать (кого-л. сделать что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ We shall need to borrow more money to keep up the payments on the house; can you get round your parents again?[/ex]
	\[[com]V3 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I think I can get round my father to lend us the car.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 get round the table садиться или сажать за стол переговоров:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ When government and unions get round the table, you can expect a long argument. The United Nations can often get two fighting nations round the table to try to agree on a peace settlement.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 get one's tongue round произносить (звуки другого языка):[/trn]
	\[[com] usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ I feel sorry for students who cannot get their tongues round some of our English consonant combinations, like "strengths".[/ex]
	
get round to
come around to
come round to
get around to
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]вернуться к (чему-л.) после перерыва:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I should be able to get round to that job next week. The committee will get round to your suggestion after they've dealt with urgent business.[/ex]
	\[[com]T4 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ After a long delay he got round to writing the letter.[/ex]
	
get somewhere
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ get anywhere, get nowhere, get there, lead nowhere
	[m1][trn]1 приходить куда-л.; приводить куда-л.:[/trn]
	\[L9 + SOMEWHERE\] [ex]♦ If you get lost in the desert, calculate your direction by the sun and keep moving, then you're sure to get somewhere.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [p]not fml[/p] приводить к успеху, помогать успеху:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ That young man shows promise, I'm sure he'll get somewhere in the end.[/ex]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Your degree should really get you somewhere in the chemical industry.[/ex]
	
get started
	[p]v adj[/p] 
	→ get cracking, get going, get moving, get weaving
	[m1][trn]становиться активным; заработать; заводить машину и т. п.; начинать (новое дело и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ After I've been on holiday, I find it hard to get started again.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I couldn't get the car started this morning, it was so cold. Getting a business started in these hard times is almost impossible. My uncle gave me a small sum of money to get me started and then I built up the business myself.[/ex]
	
get there
	[p]v adv[/p] 
	→ get anywhere, get nowhere, get somewhere, lead nowhere
	[m1][trn]1 приезжать куда-л.; довозить, доставлять (куда-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + THERE (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ "What time does the train get there?" "About half-past three."[/ex]
	\[[com]X9 + THERE (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The bus gets me there nicely in time for work.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [p]not fml[/p] приводить к успеху, содействовать процветанию:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ It was risky starting the business, but we were sure that it could get there, and we were proved right.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1b (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ You need influence to get you there, especially if you're not a businessman.[/ex]
	
get through
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 проходить, пробираться сквозь, через что-л.; пролезать куда-л.:[/trn]
	\[L9 + THROUGH\] [ex]♦ The snow was so deep that the climbers could not get through to the hut. Can you get through into the cave?[/ex]
	\[[com]X9 + THROUGH (often simple tenses)[/ex][/com]\] [ex]♦ No, but we were able to get this pipe through and talk to the trapped miners.[/ex]
	→ come through1 (1), go through1 (1) 
	[m1][trn]2 [i](о чём-л. нежелательном)[/i] проникать, просачиваться:[/trn]
	\[L9 + THROUGH\] [ex]♦ You didn't mend the roof very well, the rain is still getting through.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 выдержать экзамен, перейти на другой курс, в другой класс и т. п.; подготовить к экзаменам:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (to) (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ How many of your students got through? I'm pleased to report that all of you have got through to second year.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1b (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ With additional classes, I got all my students through except three whose English was the weakest.[/ex]
	→ put through1 (3)
	[m1][trn]4 проводить через парламент и т. п.; проходить через парламент и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Buy now before the tax increase gets through next week![/ex]
	\[T1b (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ We should have no difficulty getting the new law through, it has been demanded by the public for some time.[/ex] → go through1 (2), put through1 (4)
	[m1][trn]5 доходить до кого-л., попадать к кому-л.; доставлять что-л. кому-л.:[/trn]
	\[IØ (to)\] [ex]♦ The news of the successful climbing of the highest mountain got through on the very day of the Queen's crowning.[/ex] 
	[ex]♦ As long as the letters get through, I shan't mind the snowstorm.[/ex]
	\[T1b\] [ex]♦ Whatever happens, we must get these supplies through to our men on the other side of the battle.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 связаться с кем-л. по телефону и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (to) (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I tried to telephone you but I couldn't get through, the wires were down in the snowstorm. I can't get through to London, the operator keeps giving me the wrong number.[/ex]
	→ be through (2), come through1 (6), get through to (1), put through1 (2)
	[m1][trn]7 доводить что-л. до чьего-л. сознания:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1b (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The politician had difficulty getting his message through (to the crowd). I can't seem to get it through to Grandfather that he must rest.[/ex]
	\[T5\] [ex]♦ I can't get through to Grandfather that he must rest.[/ex]
	\[IØ (to) (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ It's difficult to get through to students who don't want to learn. [/ex]→ come across1 (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]8 закончить, завершить работу:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Telephone me when you get through, and we'll have dinner together.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1b (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I shall be glad to get this job through. [/ex]
	→ be over1 (2), get over1 (4), have over1 (2) 
	[m1][trn]9 get soaked/wet through промокнуть на сквозь; промочить что-л.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Goodness те, you've got wet through! Take your things off at once and come and sit by the fire. Trying to cross the river, the soldier got his gun wet through and was unable to fire it.[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 проходить через, сквозь (что-л.); протащить сквозь (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + through (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The man was so fat that he couldn't get through the doorway.[/ex]
	\[X9 + through (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ We can't get the piano through this narrow entrance, it'll have to come through the window.[/ex] → go through2 (1), take through2 (1)
	[m1][trn]2 выдержать экзамен, победить в соревновании; подготовить (к сдаче экзамена, соревнованиям):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Do you think that John will ever get through his driving test? Of the world's best tennis players, only three got through the first part of the competition.[/ex]
	\[D1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ It was Quite an effort to get my weakest students through the difficult English examination. [/ex]→ put through2 (2)
	[m1][trn]3 провести или одобрить законопроект через парламент и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[D1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ We should have no difficulty getting the new law through Parliament, it has been demanded by the public for some time.[/ex] → go through2 (2), put through2 (3), take through2 (3)
	[m1][trn]4 выживать, выдерживать (трудные времена):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I don't know how poor people get through these cold winters. If we can get through the next year on your income, things should be easier after that.[/ex] → go through2 (13)
	[m1][trn]5 покончить, разделаться с работой и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I've a pile of papers to get through before the meeting.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 много съесть, выпить и т. п., изрядно потратиться:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Jim gets through a lot of beer while watching football on television every Saturday. How did you get through all that money in such a short time? [/ex]
	→ go through2 \[IØ\]
	[m1][trn]7 get it through one's (thick) head/skull [p]not fml[/p] понять, представить себе:[/trn]
	\[[com] usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ I never could get it through my thick head how a car engine works.[/ex]
	
get through to
	[p]v adv
	v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 связаться с (кем-л.) по телефону и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I can't get through to Omdon, the lines are all busy.[/ex]
	→ be through (2), come through1 (6), get through1 (5, 6), put through1 (2)
	[m1][trn]2 доводить что-л. до чьего-л. сознания:[/trn]
