	[m1][trn]3 get under one's skin [p]infml[/p] раздражать кого-л., действовать на нервы:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ His endless complaints really get under my skin. "I've got you under my skin."[/ex] (song) - I can't forget you. 
	[m1][trn]4 get under way отправиться в путь, часто после задержки; начать проводить в жизнь; пускать в ход:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ After many delays, the ship at last got under way. Let's get under way, we're already late. It took a lot of hard work to get the business under way.[/ex]
	
get up
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 поднимать(ся), взбираться, залезать (куда-л. ); толкать вверх:[/trn]
	\[L9 + UP (on) (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The hill was so steep that the old car had difficulty getting up. She doesn't know how to ride a horse; in fact, she can't even get up on one without falling off. You'll have to get up on the table to change the light.
	\[[/ex]X9 + UP (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Since you can't walk up stairs after your operation, I've fixed a machine on the stairs to get you up. If everybody pushes, we'll get the car up.[/ex] → go down1 (1), go up1 (1), go up to (1), send up1 (1), take up (3)
	[m1][trn]2 вставать, подниматься с места и т. и.:[/trn]
	\[L9 + UP\] [ex]♦ When he didn't get up, I knew that he was dead. Get up when the king enters the room. Few men these days get up to give a lady a seat on a bus. Then up got Jim, all ready to attack the speaker.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 выдёргивать, вытаскивать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]X9 + UP (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ We shall have to get the tree up by its roots so that it can't grow again.[/ex]
	→ pull up (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]4 соорудить, воздвигнуть (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]X9 + UP (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Help me to get the Christmas lights up before the family come. Even the fastest builders can't get a new house up in less than a year.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 поднимать(ся), вставать, особ. утром; будить кого-л.:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ John hates getting up early. Up you get — you'll be late for work![/ex]
	\[T1b (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Please get me up at 7.30 tomorrow.[/ex] → be up (4), get up to (3), get up with (1)
	[m1][trn]6 поднимать(ся) с постели после болезни:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Mother is much better but the doctor says she's not to get up yet.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1b (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ This letter will get him up faster than any medicine.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 увеличиваться, усиливаться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ There's a wind getting up, I hope the boats are safe. [/ex]
	→ go down1 (7)
	[m1][trn]8 зарождаться, появляться [i](о чувстве)[/i]:[/trn]
	\[Tia\] [ex]♦ I can't get up any pity for the difficulties of such people, it's their own fault. I doubt if I shall ever get up any keenness for the game again. Don't mention the letter to her, I don't want her to get up any false hopes. [/ex]
	→ work up (3)
	[m1][trn]9 [p]not fml[/p] подготовить, выучить (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[Tia\] [ex]♦ How quickly can you get up this piece for the concert? I have to get up a talk for the club's next meeting. I must go and get up my notes ready for the test.[/ex] work up (5)
	[m1][trn]10 [p]not fml[/p] организовать, подготовить (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[Tia\] [ex]♦ The children are getting up a play for next week. The church is getting up a sale to collect money for the homeless children.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]11 оформить (книгу и т. п.), подготовить (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The printers have got the book up very nicely. I can get up this dress so that it's fit for the party. [/ex]
	—get-up [p]n[/p]
	[m1][trn]12 наряжать(ся), одевать(ся):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Mary got herself up in a nice new dress. The children are got up as ghosts. [/ex]
	\[IØ (simple tenses) \] [ex]♦ I shan't get up too much, the party is very informal.[/ex] 
	—get-up [p]n[/p] 
	—got-up [p]adj[/p] → dress up (2), etc., (3)
	[m1][trn]13 [i](о мяче в крикете)[/i] отскакивать от земли:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The ball is getting up and is difficult to hit.[/ex] → come through1 (5)
	[m1][trn]14 [i](команда лошади)[/i] пошла!, но!, быстрее!:[/trn]
	\[IØ (imper.)\] → come up (22), gee up, giddy up
	[m1][trn]15 get up а/one's appetite/thirst нагулять аппетит, почувствовать голод/жажду:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ I've got up such a thirst playing tennis. Swimming gets up your appetite, so you eat more and don't lose weight.[/ex]
	→ work up (7)
	[m1][trn]16 get up an appetite for [p]not fml[/p] получать удовольствие (от чего-л.); хотеть (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Early in the morning I find it difficult to get up an appetite for the day's activities. I have to make an effort to get up an appetite for teaching every day.[/ex]
	→ work up (8)
	[m1][trn]17 get someone's back/hackles up [p]infml[/p] раздражать кого-л., вывести кого-л. из себя, разозлить:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Your mother gets my back up with her endless complaints. It gets my hackles up, the way she is so unfair to the girls who work for her.[/ex] → put up1 (20)
	[m1][trn]18 get someone's blood up [p]infml[/p] сильно рассердить, разозлить кого-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ His rudeness got my blood up and I hit him in the face.[/ex]
	→ be up (14)
	[m1][trn]19 get one's dander/monkey up [p]infml[/p] рассердиться, разозлиться:[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Don't get your dander up over one little remark, she didn't mean what she said. [/ex]→ have up (3), put up1 (25)
	[m1][trn]20 get up on one's hind legs [p]infml[/p] подняться и выступить:
	[ex]♦ I'm tired of waiting for the Minister to get up on his hind legs, the newspaper will have to go without his report.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]21 get up steam:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а [i](о паровом двигателе)[/i] развести пары:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ One of the best sounds, to a train-lover, is that of a railway engine getting up steam,[/ex] 
	[m1][trn]b [p]infml[/p] поторапливаться, пошевеливаться:[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ If we want the meal on time, we'd better get up steam with the cooking. Can't you get up enough steam to get out of bed in the morning?[/ex]
	→ work up (9)
	[m1][trn]22 get the wind up esp. [p]BrE infml[/p] испугаться:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Don't get the wind up, it's only me. I'm sorry if I gave you a shock! Facing his first battle, the young soldier got the wind up and wanted to run away; but didn't want to be thought a coward, so he pretended to be brave. [/ex]
	→ have up (5), put up2 (3).
	
get up against
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 стоять около, поставить у, возле (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + UP + against (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I got up against the window to try to see out.[/ex]
	\[[com]X9 + UP + against (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Get the prisoners up against the wall.[/ex]
