	[m1][trn]1 приближаться, подходить (к кому-л. или чему-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + near/NEAR + to[/com]\] [ex]♦ How soon can you teach a baby not to go near (to) the fire? let's go near (to) the front so that we can hear the speaker more clearly.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 быть очень близким (к чему-л.) или похожим (на что-л.);[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ This film goes near (to) the quality of good old films.[/ex]
	\[[com]T4 (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I never seem to go near (to) winning any money on these games.[/ex]
	
go off
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 уходить, уезжать, особ. внезапно:[/trn]
	\[L9 + OFF (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ She went off in a bad temper. Why did the painter leave his family and go off to live on a tropical island? That dog's gone off again! Don't go off without saying goodbye, will you? Are you ready, children? Off we go, then![/ex] → go away (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 [i] (об актёре)[/i] уходить co сцены:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + OFF (usu. simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ At the end of this scene, the murderer goes off, hearing the police arrive.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 прекращаться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The pain went off after three treatments. The effect of the drug will go off after two hours.[/ex]
	→ go away (3), pass away (2), pass off (1), take away (3), wear off (3)
	[m1][trn]4 отключаться, не подаваться, не обеспечиваться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The power went off in several parts of the country during the high wind. You can't light the gas, it's gone off again. The light went off as the policemen entered the room.[/ex]
	→ put off1 (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]5 шуметь; взрываться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ A gun goes off every day to mark exactly one o'clock. I'm sorry I'm late; I overslept because my alarm clock didn't go off. [/ex]
	→ let off1 (2), set off (3), spark off (1), touch off (1). trigger off (1)
	[m1][trn]6 проходить, удаваться:[/trn]
	\[L9 (simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ How did your play go off? It went off very well, thank you. The wedding went off as planned.[/ex]
	→ bring off (2), carry off1 (6), come off1 (5, 6), pass off (2), pull off1 (4)
	[m1][trn]7 ухудшаться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The quality of performance has gone off since last year. The book goes off after the first 50 pages.[/ex]
	→ drop away (3), drop off (5), fall away (4), fall behind1 (3), fall off1 (4)
	[m1][trn]8 [p]not fml[/p] скисать или портиться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Don't drink the milk, it's gone off. I shall complain to the shop; this meat started going off the same day that I bought it.[/ex]
	→ be off1 (7), go bad
	[m1][trn]9 [p]not fml[/p] засыпать:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ I had a bad night; I went to bed early enough, but for some reason I couldn't go off. Have the children gone off yet?[/ex] → doze off, etc.
	[m1][trn]10 убежать (прихватив кого-л. или что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 (with) (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The farmer's daughter and the lawyer's son have gone off together. Let's hope they've gone off to get married. The last time this happened, the young man went off with his best friend's wife. She's gone off with my clock, the thief![/ex]
	→ abscond with, etc.
	[m1][trn]11 go off at half-cock [p]infml[/p] быть только частично успешным, удаваться наполовину:[/trn]
	\[[com] (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The government's plan to control rising prices and wages went off at half-cock when the trade unions refused to agree to the new laws.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]12 go off at a tangent [p]infml[/p] внезапно отклониться от темы:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The teacher was telling us about the poet's life, when he went off at a tangent and started talking about his own war experiences.[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 уходить (откуда-л.), оставлять (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[L9 + off\] [ex]♦ Many people think that Britain's money troubles started when she went off the gold standard. The train went off the rails and fell into the valley, killing everyone on board. The road goes off the map here. Let's go off the main road and see something of the country. His writing is so large that it looks as if it's going off the edge of the page.[/ex] → come off2 (4), take off2 (4)
	[m1][trn]2 терять интерес (к чему-л.), разлюбить (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Has Grace gone off that redhaired boy yet? I went off coffee when I was expecting a baby.[/ex]
	→ be off2 (4), put off2 (2), turn off2 (3)
	[m1][trn]3 go off the air [i] (о радиостанции)[/i] прекращать вещание:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ This station is now going off the air until tomorrow.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 go off the beaten track [p]infml[/p] сделать что-л. необычное:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ When Jim's father had been in business for ten years, he decided to go off the beaten track and make all his workers partners in the profits; as a result, he was very successful.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 go off the boil [p]infml[/p] утратить весь свой ныл, остыть:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Keep the public interested, don't let them go off the boil! After their quarrel, Jim and Mary found that their relationship had gone off the boil, and they were never again as loving as they had been.[/ex] → be on2 (9)
	[m1][trn]6 go off the deep end [p]infml[/p] говорить очень сердито, часто без причины:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ What's she going off the deep end for now? Has somebody said something wrong?[/ex] → dive off (2), jump off (2)
	[m1][trn]7 go off one's head/nut/rocker [p]infml[/p] сойти c ума:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The poet went off his head and started wandering around his house with no clothes on, in bitter cold.[/ex] → be off2 (5)
	[m1][trn]8 go off the rails [p]infml[/p] сбиться с пути истинного:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I would never have guessed that Mary of all people would go off the rails, and here she is seeing another man behind Jim's back. Young people easily go off the rails without people they respect to guide them in their behaviour.[/ex]
	→ keep on (14), run off2 (6), stay on2 (5)
		
go off into
	[p]v adv[/p]
