go on
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 продолжать путь; продолжаться, простираться:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + ON (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The police examined the cars and then allowed them to go on. Do you want to stop in this town, or shall we go on? We can't go on any further, the fall of snow has blocked the road. This range of mountains seems to go on for ever.[/ex]
	→ keep on (3)
	[m1][trn]2 идти впереди:[/trn]
	\[L9 + ON) [ex]♦ You go on, and I'll follow in a few minutes. The advance party has gone on to see if the mountain is safe for the other climbers.[/ex]
	→ go ahead (1), go forward (1)
	[m1][trn]3 налезать, надеваться:[/trn]
	\[L9 + ON (simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ No wonder this boot won't go on, I've been trying to put it on the wrong foot. If you fill the box too full, the lid won't go on.[/ex] → fit on1 (1), put on1 (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]4 вести себя; жить:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Now that our quarrel is over, can we go on as we did before? I can't go on like this, I've got to get help.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 продолжать (делать что-л.), особ. после перерыва:[/trn]
	\[IØ (to, with) (often simple tenses) \] [ex]♦ Do go on, I am listening. When you've finished those questions, go on to page 41. Why have you stopped? Go on with your work! I'd like to go on to the next piece of business.[/ex]
	\[13\] [ex]♦ After introducing the speaker, the chairman went on to give details of the meeting.[/ex]
	\[14\] [ex]♦ Do go on telling me your adventures. He just went on hitting the boy although I told him to stop! How long can anyone go on being happy?[/ex] → carry on (4), etc.
	[m1][trn]6 спешить, особ. вперёд:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Go on! There isn't a moment to lose! [/ex]
	→ get on1 (9)
	[m1][trn]7 [p]not fml[/p] говорить бесконечно долго, говорить и говорить:[/trn]
	\[IØ (about)\] [ex]♦ How she does go on! Don't keep going on so, we've all heard your story before. The teacher went on and on about good behaviour, as usual.[/ex] → be off1 (9), be on about, keep on (8), run on1 (1)
	[m1][trn]8 проходить; продолжаться, длиться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ As the weeks went on, still no letter arrived. It will get colder as the day goes on. How long did the war go on? As our marriage has gone on, we have grown closer to each other.[/ex] → fly by1 (2), go by1 (2), go past (2), pass by1 (2), run on1 (2), wear away (5), wear on1, wear out (4)
	[m1][trn]9 случаться, происходить:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (usu. continuous tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ There's a wedding going on at the church. A crowd gathered to see what was going on. Now then, what's going on here? [/ex]
	—goings-on [p]n[/p]
	[m1][trn]10 [i] (обыкн. об электрическом приборе)[/i]включаться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The street lights go on when it gets dark, and go off at midnight.[/ex]
	→ put on1 (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]11 esp. [p] NEngE[/p] продвигаться, делать успехи:[/trn] 
	\[IØ (with) (usu. continuous tenses)\] [ex]♦ How is your work going on? It's going on nicely, thank you. How is Tom going on with his new book?[/ex]→ bring on1 (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]12 esp. [p] NEngE[/p] получить (хороший) результат; удаваться:[/trn]
	\[L9\] [ex]♦ How did you go on in this morning's meeting? My students didn't go on too well in the early examination, but by the end of the year, all except three had passed.[/ex] → get on1 (6), make out (5)
	[m1][trn]13 обходиться:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 (for)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I don't know how I'd have gone on without his support. How did you go on for money while you were out of work?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]14 приближаться; [i](о времени)[/i] проходить; [i] (о людях)[/i] становиться старше:[/trn]
	\[L9 (for) (continuous tenses)\] [ex]♦ Grandfather is going on in years. Grandmother is going on for 80. It's going on for midnight, let's go to bed. I don't know her exact age, but she may be 41 going on 42. ‘This house must cost you half your monthly income.'" "Not quite, but going on that way."[/ex] → get along (4), get on1 (4), get on for, go on for
	[m1][trn]15 [p]not fml[/p] вести себя дурно или странно:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Does he often go on like that? If you go on this way, I shall have to report you.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]16 выходить на сцену или спортивную площадку:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I was so nervous when I went on.[/ex]
	→ come on1 (4), etc. 
	[m1][trn]17 иметь дружеские отношения (с кем-л.), ладить (с кем-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 (with)[/com]\] [ex]♦ We all go on well with each other here in the school. "Were all brave, they would lead a very uneasy life: all would be continually fighting; but being all cowards, we go on very well."[/ex] (James Boswell's [ex]♦ Life of Johnson)[/ex]
	→ get along (7), get along with (4), get on1 (7), get on with (3)
	[m1][trn]18 go on (with you)! [p]infml[/p] я тебе не верю!, да ну тебя!, не может быть!:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Go on with you! I know better than that! Go on! He never said that, did he?[/ex]
	→ come on1 (10), etc.
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 отправляться в путешествие:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + on[/com]\] [ex]♦ When do you go on holiday? The theatre company have gone on tour. The students are going on a special course in the mountains. I'd like to go on a long, long trip away from these troubles.[/ex]
