	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ I thought the wedding invitations had all gone out, but my sister says she hasn't received hers. The special programme on the new Prime Minister goes out tonight at 9 o'clock.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 (об [i] огне, свете и т. п.)[/i] гаснуть:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Have you a match? Му cigarette has gone out. Don't let the fire go out; there's plenty of wood. 'The lamps are going out all over Europe; we shall not see them lit again in our lifetime.'' [/ex](Lord Grey, 3 August 1914) → put out (13), etc.
	[m1][trn]6 [i] (о деньгах)[/i] быть потраченными:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ There's more money going out than coming in, and I'm worried about the business.[/ex] 
	—outgo, outgoings [p]n[/p] 
	—outgoing [p]adj[/p] → bring in (3). etc.
	[m1][trn]7 перестать быть популярным, устаревать, выйти из моды:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ I thought those ancient machines went out years ago! Formal methods of teaching are going out fast, and new ones are coming in all the time. Long skirts went out after a short season of popularity[/ex] → come in (10)
	[m1][trn]8 [i] (о периоде времени)[/i] кончаться, подходить к концу, истекать:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ March comes in like a lion and goes out like a lamb. The year went out with a terrible snowstorm.[/ex]
	→ come in (4)
	[m1][trn]9 [p] infml euph[/p] умереть:[/trn] 
	\[[com]IØ (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I hope that when I go out I shall leave a better world behind me.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 отойти от власти; выйти из соревнования:[/trn]
	\[IØ (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ If the government goes out at the next election, who will lead the country? After many victories, our team went out in the last part of the competition.[/ex] 
	—outgoing [p]adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]11 [i] (о морском отливе)[/i] начинаться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ When the sea goes out, the sand stretches for a long way.[/ex] → be in1 (10), be out (11), come in (7)
	[m1][trn]12 [p]infml[/p] потерять сознание, особ. в выражении go out like a light:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The robber hit him on the head with an iron bar, and he went out like a light. The old fighter went out for the count in the third round.[/ex]
	→ knock out (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]13 прекратить работать, забастовать:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The Post Office workers went out before Christmas, now the electricians are out; I wonder who'll come out next?[/ex] → bring out (7), etc.
	[m1][trn]14 [i](в гольфе)[/i] сыграть половину лунок на поле:[/trn] 
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Mr Ulack and his partner are still going out; I don't know what time they'll finish.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]15 go (all) out (for) [p]infml[/p] использовать малейшую возможность (чтобы получить что-л.), стараться изо всех сил:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The leading horse is going all out, and is sure to win! The young politician is going out for all the votes he can get. The factory will have to go all out for trade if it is not to fail in these hard times.[/ex]
	→ be out for.
	
go out of
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 выходить, уходить, уезжать:[/trn]
	\[L9 + OUT + of\] [ex]♦ Just as I was going out of the door, the telephone rang. You will have to go out of the country to find better weather.[/ex] → be out of (1), etc., go out (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 уходить, уменьшаться:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The anger went out of her voice when the child began to cry. All the force has gone out of his speeches recently. Most of the fun seems to have gone out of life since the children left home. When she read the letter, the colour went out of her face.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 go out of action прекратить работать, выйти из строя:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Three trains went out of action yesterday when the severe cold ruined the engines.[/ex] → put out (23), etc.
	[m1][trn]4 go out of business потерпеть крах:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Jim's father's firm will never go out of business, as trade is good and he treats his workers so well. In these hard times, even some small banks have gone out of business.[/ex] → put out (24), put out of (3)
	[m1][trn]5 go out of date устаревать, выходить из моды:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Your methods have long gone out of date; you will have to modernize or risk failure. The worst of interesting clothes is that they go out of date so quickly.[/ex]
	→ be up to (7), etc.
	[m1][trn]6 go out of one's depth:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а пытаться плавать на большой глубине:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The sea is quite safe to swim in as long as you don't go out of your depth,[/ex] 
	[m1][trn]b [p]not fml[/p] считать что-л. слишком трудным:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ You'll be going out of your depth if you apply for the director's job; try a lower position.[/ex] → be out of (8), get out of (20)
	[m1][trn]7 go out of earshot уйти из пределов слышимости, отойти в сторону:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I want to tell you something privately; let's go out of earshot of all these people at the party.[/ex]
	→ come within (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]8 go out of fashion устареть, выйти из моды:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Formal teaching methods went out of fashion some time ago, and new ideas are coming in all the time. When did long skirts go out of fashion? Loud music with a strong beat is going out of fashion, and sweeter, softer, music is becoming popular.[/ex] → come in (10), etc.
	[m1][trn]9 go out of focus [i] (о глазах или объективе и т. п.)[/i] не видеть чётко; не давать чёткого изображения:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I don't like taking those drugs, they make my eyes go out of focus and I can't see clearly. If you move too far away, the camera will go out of focus and the photograph will be spoilt.[/ex] → get out of (22)
	[m1][trn]10 go out of one's mind [p]not fml[/p] терять рассудок от беспокойства и т. п.; сходить с ума:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I'm so glad you've come; I was going out of my mind with worry. I shall go out of my mind if I teach that class for another day! It's very sad; his wife went out of her mind and had to be locked up in a special hospital. Have you gone out of your mind, letting the baby play with matches?[/ex] → be in2 (29), be out of (18) 
	[m1][trn]11 go out of someone's mind [p]not fml[/p] начисто стереться из памяти:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I don't remember his name — it's gone clean out of my mind.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]12 go out of play [i] (о мяче)[/i] выйти за пределы площадки:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ If the ball goes out of play, the other team wins a point.[/ex]
	→ be in (27a), be out of (21)
	[m1][trn]13 go out of sight/view исчезать из вида:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I waved my handkerchief until the train went out of sight, and then went sadly home. [/ex]→ keep in (16), etc.
	[m1][trn]14 go out of one's way изо всех сил стараться (делать что-л.):[/trn] 
	\[(often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ She always goes out of her way to please her guests. It was good of you to go out of your way to meet us at the station. Please don't go out of your way for us.[/ex]
	
