go over
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 проходить, пролетать, двигаться над чем-л. или кем-л.:[/trn]
	\[L9 + OVER) [ex]♦ Keep your head down, there are bullets going over. The crowd cheered as the horse went over safely.[/ex] → come over1 (1)
	[m1][trn]2 упасть; перевернуться, опрокинуться:[/trn]
	\[L9 + OVER\] [ex]♦ Look out, the wall's going over! After the crash, the car went over three times before landing upside down with the wheels spinning. Keep still or the boat will go over![/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 нанести кому-л. визит, подойти к кому-л., чтобы повидаться или поговорить:[/trn]
	\[IØ ((о)\] [ex]♦ When she saw her sister in the station, she went over to talk to her. When are we going over to your mother's again? Shall we go over and join Jim and Mary? They seem to be having an interesting conversation with their former teacher.[/ex] bring over (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]4 быть принятым, особ. с одобрением; иметь успех:[/trn]
	\[L9 (WELL, with) (usu. simple tenses) \] [ex]♦ How did your speech go over (with the crowd)? Jim went over well with Mary's parents on his first visit. This sort of music goes over well with younger people.[/ex] → come across1 (2), etc.
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 проходить, пролетать, двигаться (над чем-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + over[/com]\] [ex]♦ The crowd cheered as the horse went safely over the last fence. It's dangerous here, with bullets going over our heads all the time.[/ex]
	→ go above (1), come over2 (1)
	[m1][trn]2 осматривать (что-л. или кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ We went over the house thoroughly before buying it. Go over all the prisoners to see that they're not hiding any weapons. The doctor went over the girl carefully but could find no broken bones. You can't have the car back until it's been gone over by the police.[/ex] 
	—going-over [p]n[/p]
	→ go through2 (7), look over2 (5), run over2 (5), run through2 (7), take over2 (3), take through2 (2) 
	[m1][trn]3 просматривать (что-л.), проверять (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The police went over his story in detail, but he seemed to have been telling the truth. We must go over the account books together; there's still some money missing. [/ex]
	—going over [p]n[/p] → go through2 (9), look over2 (3)
	[m1][trn]4 чистить или ремонтировать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I've asked the garage people to go over my car thoroughly before I start my long journey,[/ex] 
	—going-over [p]n[/p]
	[m1][trn]5 делать (что-л.) снова; делать (что-л.) поверх (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Go over the stitches to make sure that they're firm. When you're satisfied that the spacing is correct, go over the letters in ink. This paint should be gone over with a new brush in the other direction.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 повторять; заучивать:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ He keeps going over the same story although no one believes him. This teacher goes over the same lesson time and time again. Let's go over that scene again until you're sure you know it. Shall we go over your English exercises together? I want to go over the arrangements for the wedding to make sure that everything will be ready on time.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 рассматривать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I've gone over your suggestion, and have decided not to accept it.[/ex] 
	—going over [p]n[/p] → look over2 (6)
	[m1][trn]8 переходить, превышать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Your spending should not go over your income. Don't go over the limit of your allowance.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 go over someone's head быть слишком трудным для кого-л., быть недоступным для чьего-л. понимания: 
	\[(usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Some of the jokes were rather rude, but luckily they went over the children's heads.[/ex] → go above (2)
	[m1][trn]10 go over someone's head действовать через голову непосредственного начальника: 
	[ex]♦ If you think that your captain is giving wrong orders, you may have to go over his head to his commanding officer, to ask what should be done.[/ex]
	→ go above (3)
	[m1][trn]11 go over the top [p]infml[/p] предпринимать что-л. отчаянное или рискованное; вести себя необузданно: 
	[ex]♦ I was told that he'd gone over the top and accepted the offer of a job in Australia.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]12 go over the wall [p]infml[/p] сбежать из тюрьмы: 
	[ex]♦ Three prisoners went over the wall last night; I don't know how they got out.[/ex]
	
