	[m1][trn]1 иметь (что-л.) против (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]D1 (simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ What do you have against Mr Brown? Don't you think he'll make a good director? I've nothing against Jim as a person, but I just don't think that he's suitable as a husband for Mary.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 быть помехой (для кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]D1 (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ It's not easy to give a man a job when he has his time in prison against him.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 have a grudge against не любить; относиться недоброжелательно (к кому-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Some employers seem to have a grudge against women; even when they give them work, they pay them less,[/ex] also bear against.
	
have at
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [p] old use[/p] атаковать (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., usu. imper.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ "Have at you!" shouted the swordsman, striking his opponent.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 have a bash/crack/go/shot/stab/try at [p]infml[/p] сделать попытку (участвовать в соревновании или выполнить что-л. трудное):[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Is Jim going to have a bash at the 500-metre race? See if you can have a crack at geting this nail out, I can't move it.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 have something at one's fingertips знать как свои пять пальцев; с лёгкостью делать что-л.; всегда располагать необходимой информацией:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ As a chairman, he's remarkable; he always has the necessary facts at his fingertips.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 have something at heart быть глубоко заинтересованным в чём-л., быть преданным чему-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ In offering these suggestions for improvement, I must explain that I only have your interests at heart, believe me.[/ex]
	
have away
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ have off (6)
	[m1][trn]have it away [p] sl[/p] иметь тайную или незаконную интимную связь (с кем-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] often continuous tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Their marriage broke up when Elizabeth discovered her husband and his secretary having it away behind her back. I suppose you've been having it away with your friend while I've been out of town![/ex]
	
have back
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 вернуть (что-л.), получить (что-л.) назад:[/trn]
	\[[com]Х9 + BACK (simple tenses')[/com]\] [ex]♦ Please can we have our ball back?[/ex]
	→ get back (4), give back (1), hand back (1)
	[m1][trn]2 позволить вернуться (обыкн. жене или мужу), принять назад (обыкн. жену или мужа):[/trn] 
	\[Х9 + BACK (simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ It was very sad; Elizabeth, after a year's separation, decided to return to her husband, but he refused to have her back.[/ex] → send back, take back (4)
	[m1][trn]3 пригласить (кого-л.) в ответ:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ We owe the Millers a dinner invitation; when are we going to have them back?[/ex] → ask back
	[m1][trn]4 have one's own back отплатить той же монетой, ответить тем же:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Не pushed те into the water, but later I had my own back when I stole his boots.[/ex] → get even, etc.
	
have by
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]иметь (ребёнка) от (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + by (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ How many children did Elizabeth have by her second husband?.[/ex]
	
have down
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 пригласить (кого-л.) в гости, обыкн. за городом:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ We must have Ijord and lady Redcliff down for our next house party.[/ex]
	→ have up (1)
	[m1][trn]2 have one's tail down [p]infml[/p] быть в подавленном состоянии:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Our team really have their tails down since they lost so many games, and without the spirit to win, look like being defeated for the whole season. [/ex]
	→ have up (4).
	
have for
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 испытывать или проявлять (что-л.) по отношению (к чему-л. или кому-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + for (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I think you should have more consideration for your family's feelings. Have you no regard for your own honour? Every mother has great fears for her children. [/ex]
	→ have about2 (3), have on2 (2)
	[m1][trn]2 иметь (что-л.) на обед и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + for[/com]\] [ex]♦ We're having fish for dinner tonight.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 пригласить (кого-л.) на обед и т. п.:[/trn] 
	\[[com]Х9 + for[/com]\] [ex]♦ When shall we have the Millers for dinner?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 be had for быть полученным (за что-л., какую-л. цену и т. п.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Good jobs like this one can't be had for the asking. Anything in the market can be had for the right price. Some good things in life can be had for nothing.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 have an appeal/attraction/fascination for привлекать (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Rare books have a great fascination for librarians. I can't imagine what appeal that boy has for Grace, but she can't leave him alone.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 have a distaste for испытывать отвращение (к чему-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Well-educated women often have a distaste for housework.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 have an ear/eye/nose for уметь разбираться в качествах, тонкостях (чего-л.), знать толк (в чём-л. ):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Any singer needs to have an ear for music. Your uncle has always had an eye for a pretty girl. As a writer, Tom has a good nose for an interesting story with a surprising ending.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 have no fears/terrors for быть лёгким (для кого-л.), не вызывать страха или трудностей (у кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Languages have no terrors for me, I can speak six and learn others easily. The dark has no fears for a blind person.[/ex] → have of (4), hold for (3)
	[m1][trn]9 have a flair/gift for иметь способность, дар (к чему-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Jim's father declared that the secret of his success was simply having a flair for business. Any successful hostess needs to have a gift for making people feel comfortable.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 have a liking/taste for любить (что-л. или кого-л.), иметь пристрастие (к чему-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Our dog had a liking for cheese. Mary has a taste for old-fashioned furniture.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]11 have a soft/weak spot for [p]not fml[/p] выделять (кого-л.), испытывать слабость (к кому-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Although they separated years ago, Grace still has a soft spot for that red-haired boy. Many mothers have a weak spot for their youngest child.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]12 have no time/use for [p]not fml[/p] быть невысокого мнения (относительно чего-л. или кого-л.), тратить время (на кого-л. или что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ I've по time for that student, he's a lazy worker and holds worthless views. This firm has no use for workers who are regularly late! I've no time for such old- fashioned methods, we must modernize at once![/ex]
	[m1][trn]13 have a weakness for очень любить (что-л.), испытывать слабость, пристрастие (к чему-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ I have such a weakness for houses with towers on the roof, I would buy one even if it were not in good condition. Peter has a weakness for fast sports cars, although he can't afford them.[/ex]
	
have in
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 располагать запасом (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Do we have enough wine in for the party?[/ex]
	→ get in1 (6), keep in (7)
	[m1][trn]2 вызывать, приглашать (кого-л.) на дом для какой-л. работы:[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ We are having the builders in next month to improve the kitchen. [/ex]
	→ bring in (5), call in (4), come in (14), get in1 (8)
	[m1][trn]3 пригласить (кого-л.) в гости:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Would you like to have your friends in for a few drinks?[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 have all one's eggs in one basket [p]not fml[/p] рисковать всем, вложить все свои деньги во что-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Insurance firms generally spread the risk among several companies so that they don't have all their eggs in one basket.[/ex]
	→ place in (8), put in2 <13>
	[m1][trn]2 have faith in доверять (кому-л. или чему-л.), верить (в кого-л. или во что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ The voters have just shown that they have no faith in the government. You must learn to have faith in your own abilities. People are leaving the Church when they find that they no longer have any faith in God.[/ex]
	→ believe in (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 have а/one's finger in every pie [p]infml[/p] заниматься многими вещами, вести активную деловую жизнь, иметь касательство ко многим сферам общественной и деловой жизни:[/trn]
	 [ex]♦ John seems to have a finger in every pie: he owns a business in the town, he's a member of the local council, and he belongs to several societies and clubs. A clever businessman is one who spreads his interests; Mr Sharp, for example, has his finger in every pie in the city.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 have a hand/part in приложить руку (к чему-л.), разделить ответственность (за что-л.):[/trn] 
	\[usu. simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Some of the servants also had a hand in the robbery. The chairman claimed that he had no part in the agreement. The radio had a hand in forming my interest in music.[/ex] → play in2 (4), take in2 (9)
	[m1][trn]5 have something in hand уже заниматься чем-л.; начать делать что-л.:[/trn] 
	\[simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Your complaint about the washing machine is being attended to; Head Office have the matter in hand. The builders have the kitchen improvements in hand and hope to be finished by next month.[/ex] → be in2 (16), take in2 (10)
	[m1][trn]6 have one's heart in очень любить (что-л.), страстно увлекаться (чем-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ \[simple tenses\] It's no good learning the piano if you don't have your heart in it.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 have one's heart in one's boots испытывать чувство страха, неуверенности и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ As I went into the examination room, I had my heart in my boots.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 have no hesitation in не испытывать ни малейших сомнений, не колебаться ни секунды:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ We have no hesitation in accepting your most generous offer.[/ex]
	→ have about2 (4)
	[m1][trn]9 have many irons in the fire [p]not fml[/p] заниматься многими делами одновременно, иметь множество планов одновременно:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ I've asked my uncle if he can help me get a job, but I've got many other irons in the fire as well, so it won't really matter if he can't find anything. Jim's father was not worried about the loss of that contract, as he had many other irons in the fire and would not lose too much business by it.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 have it in one иметь способность (делать что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ No one was surprised when Jim passed the examination; we always knew he had it in him. Do you think that Mary has it in her to be a good mother?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]11 have something in mind думать о чём-л., иметь что-л. на уме, намереваться сделать что-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ What plans for your future do you have in mind? At one time I had it in mind to study law.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]12 have a place in занимать какое-л. место (где-л., в чём-л.):[/trn]
	\[simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Our first home will always have a special place in our hearts. Training for a job has little place in general education.[/ex] → figure in2
	[m1][trn]13 have someone in one's power/spell иметь над кем-л. магическую власть; подчинить кого-л. себе словно при помощи магии:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Soon the speaker had the whole crowd in his spell. Their leader has the whole trade union movement in his power.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]14 have a say/voice in быть в состоянии принять участие в обсуждении (чего-л.), иметь право голоса:[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Jim felt that he was old enough to have a say in his father's business. By giving their votes at elections, the taxpayers think that they actually have a voice in running the country.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]15 have a stake in иметь долю (в чём-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Jim has a small stake in his father's firm, so it is in his own interests to help it to be successful. Most parents feel that they have a stake in their children's future, and so will use every effort to help them.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]16 have something well in hand легко справляться (с чем-л., напр. с затруднением), держать под контролем (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ The government claims that it will soon have rising prices well in hand.[/ex]
	→ be in2 (34)
	[m1][trn]17 have one's tongue in one's cheek [p]not fml[/p] говорить иронически, насмешливо, издевательски:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Don't be offended by what he said; can't you tell when he has his tongue in his cheek?.[/ex]
	
have it
	[m0][p]v pron[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [p]infml[/p] износиться, устареть, устать:[/trn]
