	[m1][trn]1 держать(ся) в стороне (от чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + off[/com]\] [ex]♦ Keep off the grass.[/ex]
	\[X9 + off\] Can't you keep your dog off the road? Keep your dirty fingers off the wet paint. At least the club keeps the young people off the streets.[/ex] → get off2 (2), stay off (1)
	[m1][trn]2 избегать; удерживать(ся) (от пищи, привычки и т, п.):[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ The doctor advised Jim to keep off fattening foods[/ex]
	\[D1\] [ex]♦ How did you keep your husband off alcohol all last year?[/ex]
	→ keep away from (2), stay away from, stay off (2)
	[m1][trn]3 избегать (какой-л. темы):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I suggest that you keep off religion while the priest is here. Remember to keep off the delicate subject of Jane's stomach trouble in her hearing.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 не пускать (кого-л. в школу и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[D1\] [ex]♦ I shall keep Jane off school until her stomach trouble is better.[/ex]
	→ stay off (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]5 [b]keep one's eyes off[/b] оторвать взгляд (от че- го-л. или кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. neg., usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ The crowd could not keep their eyes off the beautiful young singer. The thief found it difficult to keep his eyes off the jewels round Miss Rose's neck, as he planned to steal them later that night.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 [b]keep one's fingers/hands off[/b] не трогать (кого-л. или что-л.), держаться подальше (от кого-л. или чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Keep your hands off my precious paintings while I'm away. He learned the hard way to keep his hands off other men's wives. Rig business organizations cannot keep their hands off successful small firms.[/ex]
	→ lay off2 (4)
	
keep on
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 не снимать (одежды):[/trn]
	\[X9 + ON) [ex]♦ I'll keep my coat on, thank you, I can't stay long. It's rude for a man to keep his hat on indoors.[/ex]
	→ leave on1 (2)
	[m1][trn]2 держать (свет) включённым:[/trn]
	\[X9 + ON\] [ex]♦ There is no need for tall office buildings to keep their lights on all night, when the government has asked everyone to save power.[/ex]
	→ put on1 (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 продолжать двигаться:[/trn]
	\[L9 + ON (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Turn left at the corner and keep on as far as the church. "Keep right on to the end of the road."[/ex] (song) → go on1 (1)
	[m1][trn]4 продолжать делать что-л., несмотря на трудности:[/trn] 
	\[[com]IØ (with) (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Even if you fail the examination again, you must try to keep on until you pass. Keep on with your studies, however hard it sometimes seems. I've never won anything yet, but I intend to keep on until I do.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 продолжать (делать что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T4a (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ A string broke, but the pianist kept on playing. He just kept on hitting the boy although I told him to stop.[/ex]→ carry on (4), etc.
	[m1][trn]6 продолжать держать (кого-л. на работе, в школе и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[Т1 (at, in)\] [ex]♦ Were you able to keep both the gardeners on? How long will you keep the children on at that school after all that you've heard about it?[/ex] → stay on1 (3), stick on1 (4), stop on (3)
	[m1][trn]7 продолжать пользоваться (чем-л.), особ. взятым внаем:[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ I'll keep the flat on through the summer, so as to have it ready when we come back. I'd like to keep this car on for another week, if that's possible.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 [p]not fml[/p] долго рассуждать (о чём-л.):[/trn]
	\[IØ (about) (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Don't keep on so, it'll only make you worry more. The teacher kept on and on about good behaviour, as usual.[/ex] → be off1 (9), be on about, go on1 (7), run on1 (1)
	[m1][trn]9 keep your hair/shirt on [p]infml[/p] не теряй головы; сохраняй спокойствие:[/trn]
	\[[com]usu imper. [/com]\] [ex]♦ All right, keep your hair on! Nobody's blaming you![/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 удерживать(ся) на верху (чего-л. или кого-л. ):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + on (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ How do you keep on a horse?[/ex]
	\[X9 + on\] [ex]♦ She kept her hat on her head with a long pin.[/ex]
	→ be on2 (1), stay on2 (1)
	[m1][trn]2 продолжать путь (в определённом направлении), держать(ся) (в определённом направлении):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + on (simple tenses')[/com]\] [ex]♦ If we keep on our way for another hour we should reach the village.[/ex]
	\[X9 + on\] [ex]♦ The captain was able to keep the ship on (her) course in spite of the storm.[/ex]
	→ be on2 (2), remain on2 (1), stay on2 (2)
	[m1][trn]3 продлевать (приём лекарств, курс лечения и т. п.):[/trn] 
	\[Х9 + on\] The doctor wants to keep Mother on the medicine for another month.
	\[[/ex]L9 + on\] [ex]♦ Will you keep on the course of exercises until you are thinner?[/ex]
	→ be on2 (4), stay on2 (3)
	[m1][trn]4 [b]keep someone on the boil[/b] [p]infml[/p] поддерживать интерес в ком-л.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ With violent words and fierce expressions, the speaker kept the angry crowd of workers on the boil long enough to persuade the directors to give them their demands.[/ex]
	→ be on2 (9), go off2 (5)
	[m1][trn]5 [b]keep an/one's eye on[/b] наблюдать (за кем-л. или чем-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The police have been keeping an eye on the thief ever since he came out of prison. Property owners try to keep their eyes on future developments in house prices.[/ex]
	→ have on2 (10), watch over
	[m1][trn]6 [b]keep one eye on[/b] не выпускать из поля зрения (что-л. или кого-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ It's wise to keep one eye on the clock when doing the examination. Whatever Jim was doing, he always kept one eye on his own interests. When planning anything, you have to keep one eye on the future.[/ex]
	→ have on2 (11)
	[m1][trn]7 [b]keep something on its feet[/b] [p]not fml[/p] спасать что-л. от провала, банкротства и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ This new contract should keep the business on its feet for another six months, but after that the future is uncertain.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 [b]keep on one's feet[/b] держать(ся) на ногах:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The old fighter kept on his feet in spite of punishing blows from his strong young opponent. How do you keep on your feet so long? I would have needed a rest hours ago. It's unlawful to keep workers on their feet for too long at a time.[/ex] → get to (11), etc.
	[m1][trn]9 [b]keep one's finger on the pulse of[/b] [p]not fml[/p] быть в курсе всех событий, держать руку на пульсе:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ A successful politician is one who is able to keep his finger on the country's pulse and knows what to offer the voters.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 [b]keep a firm/tight grip/hold on[/b]:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а продолжать удерживать (что-л. или кого-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Keep a firm hold on the rope and we'll pull you up. [/ex]
	[m1][trn]b продолжать держать под контролем (что-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Jim's father kept a tight hold on the business during its difficult early period, and claims that this was partly responsible for its later success.[/ex] → keep of (3a), lose of (3), take of (10)
	[m1][trn]11 [b]keep someone on the hop[/b] [p]infml[/p] не давать кому-л. покоя, заставить кого-л. ждать, побегать и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ That salesman deserves to be kept on the hop for another week, he annoyed me the last time he called, and he can wait for his sale.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]12 [b]keep something/someone on ice[/b] [p]infml[/p] держать что-л./кого-л. про запас; держать что-л./кого-л. на заметке:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ We may have to keep the contract on ice until we have the supplies to fulfil it. So many people wanted the job; I'd like to keep some of the better ones on ice to see if we can find jobs for them later.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]13 [b]keep one's mind on[/b] сосредоточиться (на чём-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ It's hard to keep your mind on your work with all this noise going on. [/ex]→ concentrate on (1), focus on (2)
	[m1][trn]14 [b]keep on the rails[/b] [p]not fml[/p] держать(ся) в рамках закона, приличий и т. п.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ It's hard for a former prisoner to keep on the rails. Having the responsibility for a family helps to keep a young man on the rails.[/ex]
	→ go off2 (8), run off2 (6), stay on2 (5)
	[m1][trn]15 [b]keep on the right side of[/b] [p]not fml[/p] оставаться в дружеских отношениях (с кем-л.); не досаждать (кому-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ It will pay you to keep on the right side of the director, as if you annoy him he could have you dismissed.[/ex]
	→ get in with (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]16 [b]keep on the right side of the law[/b] не преступать закон:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The courts will punish less severely, for a first offence, someone who has formerly kept on the right side of the law. [/ex]→ remain on2 (5), stay on2 (7)
	[m1][trn]17 [b]keep tabs on[/b] [p]infml[/p] наблюдать; отслеживать; вести учёт:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ How do you keep tabs on the accounts of all these different firms? The police keep tabs on all habitual criminals. [/ex]→ keep of (5), lose of (6)
	[m1][trn]18 [b]keep on top (of)[/trn] 
	[/b]→ stay on2 (8).
	
keep on at
keep on onto
	[p] v adv
	v prep, prep[/p]
	→ go on at (2), etc.[p] 
	[m1][trn]not fml[/p] ругать; приставать (к кому-л.), надоедать (просьбами):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ She keeps on at the children all day about one thing or another.[/ex]
	\[V3\] [ex]♦ She kept on at/onto me for a year to buy her a new coat, until in the end I agreed, if only to get some peace from her.[/ex]
	
keep open
	[p]v adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 держать (что-л.) открытым:[/trn]
	\[X9 + open\] [ex]♦ Don't keep your mouth open all the time, it looks ugly. Use this block to keep the door open, so as to let some air into the house. [/ex]→ leave open (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 оставаться открытым, продолжать работать:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + open (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The shops in central London keep open late on Thursday evenings.[/ex]
	→ stay open (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 оставлять за кем-л. (рабочее место):[/trn]
	\[T1b (/or)) [ex]♦ If you take this sixmonth overseas contract, will your firm keep your job open for you until you get back?[/ex]
	→ hold open (2) 
	[m1][trn]4 keep one's eyes open не засыпать:[/trn]
	\[[com](usu. neg.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The speaker was so uninteresting that I could hardly keep my eyes open.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 [b]keep one's eyes (and cars) open[/b] [p]not fml[/p] быть бдительным, держать глаза открытыми:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Keep your eyes open for a jewel thief in the neighbourhood. Any businessman has to keep his eyes and ears open for a chance of making a profit.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 [b]keep one's options open[/b] [p]not fml[/p] быть уверенным, что есть выбор; не делать поспешного выбора:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Don't leave your present job until you have actually accepted the one that you've been offered, just in case you decide not to take it; that way you will be keeping your options open.[/ex]
	
keep out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ stay out (1), stop out (1) 
	[m1][trn]держать(ся) вне чего-л.; не входить:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + OUT (of) \] [ex]♦ This notice should keep unwanted visitors out. Don't keep your aunt out in the cold, ask her to come in! Warm clothing helps to keep out the cold.[/ex]
	\[[com]L9 + OUT (of) (usu. simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ The children have been warned to keep out of the fields while the crops are growing. Private property — keep out.[/ex]
	
keep out of
	[p]v adv
	v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 держать(ся) вне (чего-л.); не входить (куда-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]Х9 + OUT + of[/com]\] [ex]♦ This notice should keep unwanted visitors out of the building.[/ex]
	\[L9 + OUT + of (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The children have been warned to keep out of the fields while the crops are growing.[/ex] → stay out of (1), stop out of (2)
	[m1][trn]2 держать(ся) в стороне (от чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I always try to keep out of other people's affairs when they don't concern me. I hope you'll keep out of trouble while I'm away. It's not easy to keep out of debt when prices are rising so fast.[/ex]
	\[D1\] [ex]♦ I hope you'll keep him out of trouble while I'm away. This recent pay rise will help to keep us out of debt a little longer.[/ex]
	→ get into (11), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 keep (someone) out of harm's way держаться) в безопасности, держать(ся) от греха подальше:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The children don't do much at their youth club, but at least it keeps them out of harm's way.[/ex]
	→ be out of (12), stay out of (3)
	[m1][trn]4 keep one's nose out of [p]not fml[/p] не совать свой нос в чужие дела:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Keep your nose out of my affairs![/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 keep out of one's/the way не мешать кому-л.; не беспокоить кого-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com](usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Keep out of my way, can't you — I need room to get past![/ex]
	→ be out of (32a), get out of (37), stay out of (4).
	
keep quiet
	[p]v adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 успокоить(ся), не шуметь:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + quiet (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Keep quiet, please, children. Father's asleep.[/ex]
	\[[com]X9 + quiet (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Give the dog a bone, that should keep him quiet. Some small tax cuts might keep the voters quiet.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 держать (сведения) в секрете:[/trn] 
	\[[com]IØ (about)[/com]\] [ex]♦ All the children kept quiet about the trouble they had got into at school.[/ex]
	\[T1b\] [ex]♦ If I tell you the story, will you promise to keep it quiet?[/ex]
	→ keep dark (2), keep mum
	
keep till
keep until
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 держать (что-л. до определённого времени), хранить (что-л. до определённого времени):[/trn]
	\[Т1 + till/until\] [ex]♦ The library will let you keep the book till next week. Keep this ring until my return. The teacher kept the whole class till 4.30. [/ex]→ save till
	[m1][trn]2 не портиться, оставаться в хорошем состоянии (до определённого времени):[/trn] 
	\[IØ + till/until (simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The milk won't keep till tomorrow, we'd better drink it now.[/ex] → last for, last till
	[m1][trn]3 ждать (до определённого времени):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Writing that letter will have to keep until tomorrow. Can't your story keep until another time?[/ex]
	
keep to
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 двигаться (в каком-л. направлении), держаться (какого-л. направления):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + to (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Traffic in England keeps to the left. If you keep to the main road you won't get lost. Ask the crowd to keep to one side, to let the firemen get through.[/ex]
	→ hold to (2), stick to (2)
	[m1][trn]2 ограничиваться) (чем-л.); держать(ся) (темы), не отвлекаясь:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Do let's keep to the subject; we're trying to reach an agreement, not have a conversation.[/ex]
	\[[com]D1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ It is part of the chairman's duty to keep each speaker to the point, to save wasting time.[/ex]
	→ stick to (3) 
	[m1][trn]3 следовать (чему-л.), придерживаться (обещания или планов); заставлять придерживаться (обещания, правил и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ I'd rather keep to the original arrangement. Whatever your argument, I shall keep to my decision. The priest kept to his beliefs in spite of cruel treatment. You must keep to the rules even if you think they're unfair.[/ex]
	\[D1\] [ex]♦ I shall keep you to your promise to come and see us next year. There's nothing to keep me to such an arrangement.[/ex]
	→ adhere to (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]4 оставаться (в постели, дома и т. п ):[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ Mother had to keep to her bed for two weeks after her operation. When Jane is in a bad temper, she keeps to her room all day.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 keep one's ear (close) to the ground [p]not fml[/p] быть в курсе событий, видеть тенденции, уметь просчитать будущие результаты:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ By keeping his ear close to the ground, the young politician learned the best ways to win votes.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 keep something to a/the minimum сводить что-л. к минимуму: 
	[ex]♦ This new protection on the car is intended to keep damage to a minimum.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 keep one's nose to the grindstone [p]infml[/p] много и напряжённо работать; заставлять много и напряжённо работать:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Jim's father succeeded in business by keeping his nose to the grindstone, and never taking a holiday. Jane is lazy at school and never does any work unless the teacher keeps her nose to the grindstone.[/ex] → get back to (5)
	[m1][trn]8 keep (oneself) to oneself быть замкнутым, необщительным; сторониться других людей:[/trn] 
	(often simple tenses) [ex]♦ Nobody really knows what Mary thinks about anything, she keeps (herself) to herself most of the time, and rarely expresses an opinion.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 keep something to oneself держать (что-л.) при себе; не рассказывать (о чём-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I kept the letter to myself, for fear of offending my friends. Don't keep the news to yourself, let's all share it. I'll thank you to keep your opinion to yourself.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 keep to/on the straight and narrow (path) держать(ся) в строгих рамках; вести себя правильно, заставлять вести себя правильно:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Every Sunday, the priest tries to persuade the worshippers to keep to the straight and narrow path. It's no use trying to keep one's children to the straight and narrow path, they will do what they like with their own lives.[/ex]
	
keep together
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [i] (о двух и больше предметах или людях)[/i] держать(ся) вместе (где-л. или когда-л):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + TOGETHER (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The horses kept together for most of the race, until a leader at last came to the front. When the mist comes down, we must keep together or we'll be lost. You singers must keep together or you will spoil the music.[/ex]
	\[X9 + TOGETHER (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ I'd like to keep the children together in school. A library usually keeps books of the same kind together. How do you keep such a large number of musicians together, playing so many different instruments?[/ex] → remain together, stay together (1)
	[m1][trn]2 оставаться объединёнными; объединять:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ We try to keep together as a family.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The party was kept together by personal loyalty to the leader. Facing a common enemy keeps the nation together.[/ex]
	→ stick together (2), etc.
	
keep under
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 удерживать(ся) под чем-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com]X9 + UNDER (often simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ He tried to swim, but a strong current kept him under and he drowned.[/ex]
	→ hold under (1), remain under1, stay under, stop under
	[m1][trn]2 держать (что-л.) под контролем:[/trn]
	\[T1b\] [ex]♦ Jim kept his feelings under with an effort. We tried to keep the fire under, but is spread to the other buildings near it.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 держать под контролем; ограничивать свободу (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1b\] [ex]♦ Former rulers kept the people under, but recently they have been allowed to hold elections and have much more freedom.[/ex]
	→ keep down (6), etc.
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 держать (что-л. под чем-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + under (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ There is no room for my shoes in the cupboard, so I have to keep them under the bed.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 keep something/someone under control держать что-л./кого-л. под контролем; управлять чем-л./кем-л.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ If that fire is not kept under control, it could spread to other buildings in the street. How long can an inexperienced teacher keep a class like that under control?[/ex]
	→ be under (7), bring under2 (2), get under2 (2)
	[m1][trn]3 keep something under one's hat [p]infml[/p] держать (сведения) в секрете:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ If I tell you the date of the election, can you keep it under your hat?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 keep someone under observation:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а наблюдать за чьими-л. действиями:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The police keep all habitual criminals under observation from the day they leave the prison,[/ex] 
	[m1][trn]b обследовать кого-л. в больнице:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The doctors cannot understand Jane's stomach trouble, and they want to keep her under observation for a few days.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 keep someone under one's thumb [p]not fml[/p] держать кого-л. под башмаком, угнетать кого-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com] (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ If their mother tries to keep the children under her thumb, they will leave home as soon as they can.[/ex]
	
keep up
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 держать(ся) наверху:[/trn]
	\[[com]X9 + UP (often simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ My husband lost so much weight that he has to wear a belt to keep his trousers up. What keeps the roof up?[/ex]
	\[[com]L9 + UP (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ How does that pile of hair keep up, I wonder?[/ex]
	→ hold up (1), etc. 
	[m1][trn]2 оставаться высоким; оставлять высоким:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The farmers are keeping the prices up. She kept up her spirits by singing. The shops are trying to keep quality up but prices steady.
	\[[com][/ex]IØ (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Prices have kept up all the year. His courage kept up in the face of danger.[/ex]
	→ be up (6, 7), go down1 (6), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 содержать (что-л.) в должном порядке:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ How do you keep up a house as large as this without help? [/ex]
	—upkeep [p]n[/p]
	[m1][trn]4 продолжать (что-л.), не прекращать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I'm glad that you are keeping up your studies. Jane will have to keep up her piano practice if she wants to be a professional concert performer. 
	The enemy kept up the attack all night, until help arrived in the morning. 
	When people move to another country, they often try to keep up the customs of their native land. 
	How long Is the director going to keep up the pretence that business is still good? You'll be able to pay all your debts if you can keep up regular payments. 
	I've kept up a friendship with a girl I was at school with twenty years ago. 
	Well done! Keep up the good work! 
	You boys have done some very good work; I hope you can keep it up.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 [i] (обыкн. о погоде)[/i] длиться, продолжаться, держаться, не меняться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Will the fine weather keep up?[/ex]
	→ hang out (3), hold out (3), hold up (6), last out (1)
	[m1][trn]6 не (давать) спать допоздна; ложиться спать поздно:[/trn]
	\[T1b\] [ex]♦ I hope I'm not keeping you up.[/ex]
	\[[com]IØ (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ No, we often keep up late.[/ex]
	→ stay up (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]7 держаться наравне (с кем-л.), не отставать (от кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (to, with)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I had to run to keep up (with the girls).[/ex]
	→ catch up (4), come up with (1), get up with (2), keep up with (1)
	[m1][trn]8 не отставать (от соперника, конкурента):[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The other firms are selling so well that we have to work very hard to keep up.[/ex]
	→ catch up (5), come up with (2), keep up with (2)
	[m1][trn]9 keep up appearances делать вид, что всё хорошо; делать хорошую мину при плохой игре:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ When Father lost all his money, all that Mother could think of was keeping up appearances in the town so that people should not know of the family's shame.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 keep one's chin/pecker up [p]BrE infml[/p] не падать духом, не вешать нос; не унывать:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Keep your chin up! Things could be worse! [/ex]→ cheer up, etc.
	[m1][trn]11 keep one's end up стойко держаться, упорно защищаться, отбиваться:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The opposition offered some strong speakers, but our man kept his end up all right. I tried to keep my end up in the discussion but everyone else seemed much cleverer than I was.[/ex]
	
keep up to
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 оставаться на таком же высоком уровне:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The shops are keeping meat prices up to last month's level, although they are paying the farmers less for producing the meat.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 не отставать (от чего-л. или кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I had to run to keep up to the girls. I had to work hard to keep up to the other students.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 keep (someonc/something) up to date сохранять(ся) на современном уровне, держаться) на современном уровне, шагать в ногу со временем:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ It's always a struggle to keep up to date with the news. How do you keep your letter writing up to date? Throw away your old clothes, keep up to date![/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 keep (someone) up to the mark/up to scratch держать(ся) на высоком уровне, держать/не ронять марку:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ A pianist has to practise every day to keep up to scratch. This teacher demands hard work from his students, to keep them up to the mark.[/ex]
	
keep up with
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 не отставать от (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ I had to run to keep up with the girls. I had to work hard to keep up with the other students. [/ex]→ catch up (4), come up with (1), get up with (2), keep up1 (7), keep up2 (2)
	[m1][trn]2 быть всегда в курсе (чего-л.), быть хорошо осведомлённым (о чём-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ How do you keep up with the latest styles in popular music? They change so quickly! Everyone has to keep up with the news. Even professional politicians have a struggle to keep up with the changes in public opinion.[/ex]
	→ catch up (5), come up with (2), keep up1 (8)
	[m1][trn]3 keep up with the Joneses [p]not fml[/p] стараться быть не хуже других, не отставать от соседей, знакомых и т. д.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ One of the problems of modern city life is that people spend half their effort and income in just keeping up with the Joneses, instead of developing an independent lifestyle and spending their money only on things they need and really want.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 keep up with the times быть на уровне, шагать в ногу со временем:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Keeping up with the times can be almost a full-time job!.[/ex]
	
keep with
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 держать (что-л. с чем-л.) вместе:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + with (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Why do you keep the knives with the forks? I separate mine.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 keep company with водить дружбу с (кем-л.), появляться везде с (кем-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Is Grace still keeping company with that red-haired boy?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 keep faith with [p]fml[/p] быть верным, хранить верность (кому-л. или чему-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com](usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ When Ulysses returned from the war, he found that his wife had kept faith with him for ten long years She had kept faith with the promise that she had made to him on their marriage.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 in keeping with в соответствии с (чем-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ In keeping with the spirit of the occasion, let us close the meeting with a prayer. The employment exchange tries to find people jobs in keeping with their abilities and experience.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 keep pace/step with:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а идти в ногу с (кем-л.), поспевать за (кем-л.):[/trn]
	\[(usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The tall girl took such long steps that I had a struggle to keep pace with her.[/ex] 
	[m1][trn]b не отставать от прогресса:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Scientists have to work hard to keep pace with modern discoveries and developments.[/ex]
	
keep within
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 быть ограниченным (чем-л):[/trn] 
	\[L9 + within (usu simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Make sure you keep within the new lower speed limit
	→ remain within (1), stay within (1), stick within, stop within[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [b]keep (something) within bounds[/b] держаться в рамках, не переходить границы:[/trn] 
	\[(usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Make sure that your spending keeps within bounds, or we could be in trouble. The firm's costs must be kept within bounds on this new contract.[/ex]
	
key up
