	[m1][trn]отмечаться при уходе в специальном журнале:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ They're по longer here; I'll see if they logged out this morning.[/ex]
	
log up
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [i] (о транспортном средстве)[/i] отмечать (пройденное расстояние или время пути):[/trn]
	\[T1 + UP\] [ex]♦ The ship has now logged up voyages equal to a distance ten times round the world. A pilot in training has to log up a certain number of hours of flying alone before he is allowed to fly passengers.[/ex]
	→ clock up (1)
	[m1][trn]2 [p]not fml[/p] добиться (каких-л. показателей); отмечать, регистрировать (достижения, выигрыши и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Our team has logged up an unusual number of wins this season. [/ex]→ chalk up (3), clock up (2), notch up, score up.
	
loiter about
loiter around
	[p]v adv; prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]слоняться без дела, околачиваться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + ABOUT/ AROUND/about/around[/com]\] [ex]♦ There's a nasty-looking man loitering around (your gate); shall I call the police?[/ex]
	
loiter away
	[p]v adv[/p] 
	→ idle away (2), etc. 
	[m1][trn]попусту, зря тратить время, даром терять время:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I've another hour to loiter away before the train comes. "So have I loitered my life away, reading books, looking at pictures, going to plays, hearing, thinking, writing on what pleased me best."[/ex] (William Hazlitt, [ex]♦ Winterslow: My First Acquaintance with Poets)[/ex]
	
loiter in
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]слоняться без дела, околачиваться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + in[/com]\] [ex]♦ Please do not loiter in the streets.[/ex]
	
loiter over
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	→ linger over
	[m1][trn]бездельничать, лодырничать на работе; мешкать, медлить:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + over[/com]\] [ex]♦ You won't increase your earnings by loitering over your work.[/ex]
	
loiter with
loiter with intent
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]loiter with intent[/trn] 
	[m1][trn][p] BrE law[/p] околачиваться где-л. с подозрительной целью:[/trn]
	\[[com] often continuous tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ The man was taken to the police station on a charge of loitering with intent.[/ex]
	
loll about
loll around
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ lounge about 
	[m1][trn]сидеть развалясь, стоять в ленивой позе, распуститься:[/trn]
	\[L9 + ABOUT/AROUND\] [ex]♦ Do stand up straight, don't loll about like that![/ex]
	
loll back
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]сидеть развалясь и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[L9 + BACK\] [ex]♦ Не lolled back in his seat, enjoying the sunshine.[/ex]
	
loll out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]высовывать(ся); болтаться:[/trn]
	\[L9 + OUT\] [ex]♦ The dog's tongue was lolling out.[/ex]
	\[T1 + OUT\] [ex]♦ The dog lolled its tongue out in the heat.[/ex]
	
long for
	[m0][p]v prep[/p] 
	→ itch for, sigh for (2), yearn for
	[m1][trn]страстно желать, жаждать; стремиться:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + for[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'm longing for a cool drink, aren't you? Is this the house for which you longed so much? I hope Grace isn't going to spend the rest of her life longing for that red-haired boy. Price reductions are longed for, but unlikely. The crowd were longing for the performance to start. You seem to be longing for me to say something,[/ex] 
	-longed-for [p]adj.[/p]
	
look about
look around
	I
	[p]v adv[/p] 
	→ look round1
	[m1][trn]1 оглядываться вокруг, озираться:[/trn]
	\[IØ + ABOUT/AROUND\] [ex]♦ Looking about, I could see no sign of life.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 наводить справки, справляться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ We should look around carefully before deciding which house to buy. I'm not ready to choose yet, I'm still looking around.[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	→ look round2
	[m1][trn]1 оглядываться вокруг, озираться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + about/around[/com]\] [ex]♦ I looked around the station but couldn't see my friend anywhere. Looking about the room, I could see no sign of life. The stranger looked about him at the wonderful new sights.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 подыскивать, присматривать:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (for) (no pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ We've been looking about the country for a good place to camp. Although we've looked all around the city, we haven't found a house at a suitable price.[/ex]
	
look after
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 смотреть вслед:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + after[/com]\] [ex]♦ Sadly we looked after the last bus as it disappeared round the corner. He turned and left her; but she looked after him with tears in her eyes.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 ухаживать, присматривать за (кем-л. или чем-л.); заботиться о (ком-л. или чём-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Who will look after the children while you go out to work? Who's looking after the arrangements for the wedding? If you look after your new shoes, they will last longer. This house seems to have been well looked after. Mother was well looked after in hospital.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 esp. [p]AmE infml[/p] убить (кого-л.), расправиться с (кем-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1 (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ We can't let him live to tell stories about us to the police, leave him to me, I'll look after him. [/ex]→ bump off, etc.
	[m1][trn]4 look after oneself не нуждаться в посторонней помощи, всё уметь самому, справляться самому:[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Don't worry about me, I can look after myself.[/ex]
	
look ahead
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ see ahead
	[m1][trn]1 смотреть вперёд, вдаль:[/trn]
	\[IØ + AHEAD\] [ex]♦ If you look ahead in the distance, you can just see the lights of the village.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 предвидеть, предусматривать:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ If you want to make a success of your life, you have to learn to look ahead. When choosing a profession, it is wise to look ahead and see what kind of a future each one offers. Looking ahead to the future, we can imagine a time when all cars will run on electricity. Planning officials are now looking ahead to the needs of the population in the year 2000.[/ex]
	
look alike
	[p]v adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]быть (очень) похожими:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + alike (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The two brothers look so much alike that even their own mother can hardly tell the difference between them. All the houses in this street look alike, it's very dull,[/ex] 
	—look-alike [p]n[/p] esp. [p]AmE[/p]
	
look alive
look lively
	[p]v adj[/p]
	→ look sharp, etc. 
	[m1][trn][p]not fml[/p] поворачиваться, не зевать, действовать быстро:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (usu. imper.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Look alive, everybody, the director's coming into the office! Now men, look lively! We need some action around here![/ex]
	
look aside
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 смотреть в одну точку, в одну сторону:[/trn] 
	\[IØ + ASIDE (from) (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The singer was afraid that if she looked aside, even for a moment, she would lose her place in the music.[/ex] → look away
	[m1][trn]2 отвлекаться, отрываться (от кого-л. или чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (from) (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Once you're started in business, there's no looking aside from the work and responsibility.[/ex]
	
look askance at
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]смотреть с сомнением, подозрением:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Mother looked askance at the meal, hardly believing that her daughter could cook. Disbelieving his unlikely story, she looked askance at him.[/ex]
	
look at
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 смотреть, наблюдать, следить; смотреть на (кого-л. или что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + at[/com]\] [ex]♦ The children spend too much time looking at television. She looked at him in great surprise, wondering what he meant. My work hasn't been looked at for a week. Please don't wear that terrible suit; you'll get looked at. "A cat may look at a king, said Alice."[/ex] (Lewis Carroll, [ex]♦ Alice in Wonderland)[/ex]
	→ look on2 (1)
	[m1][trn]2 рассматривать, смотреть, считать, полагать:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Jim looks at his work in a different way now that he is in charge. Happiness depends on how you look at life. You have to look at these things as a scientist. The difficulty can now be looked at in a different light.[/ex]
	→ look on2 (2)
	[m1][trn]3 рассматривать, изучать; осматривать:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I'll look at your report tomorrow. Would you look at this painting that I've found and tell me if it's valuable? That tooth wants looking at. You'd better have your ankle looked at (by the doctor).[/ex]
	→ take at (7)
	[m1][trn]4 быть достойным внимания, представлять интерес, интересовать:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (no pass., neg., usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I wouldn't look at such a poor offer. After that meal, I can't look at any more food! Such an unsuitable arrangement is not worth looking at. After the way he treated her, she wouldn't look at another man.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 to look at по внешнему виду:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ She's not much to look at but she is very nice. I want a building that's pleasant to look at.[/ex] → look on2 (4)
	[m1][trn]6 to look at судить по внешнему виду:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ You wouldn't think, to look at him, that he was very rich, would you? To look at the house, you would never guess its age.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 look daggers at смотреть зло, с ненавистью на (кого-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ She looked daggers at me when I suggested that her cooking could be improved.[/ex]
	
look away
	[p]v adv[/p] 
	→ look aside (1)
	[m1][trn]отводить взгляд; не смотреть на (что-л. или кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + AWAY (from)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The singer was afraid that if she looked away, even for a moment, she would lose her place in the music. Look away dear, the sight is not fit for your eyes. I hate to see accidents, so I looked away as we passed the scene of the crash.[/ex]
	
look back
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 оглядываться назад:[/trn]
	\[IØ + BACK\] [ex]♦ Looking back the way we had come, we could see the whole valley spread out behind us.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 вспоминать прошлое, обращаться к прошлому:[/trn] 
	\[IØ (at, on, to\] [ex]♦ It's easy to make judgments looking back at the past. When we look back, we can now understand what caused the difficulty. Never look back, you can't relive the past. Looking back on the old days, I'm sure we were much happier then. ‘Look Back in Anger.'[/ex] (title of play and film)
	[m1][trn]3 [b]not/never look back[/b] продолжать идти вперёд, не останавливаться:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Since Jim got started in business, he hasn't looked back. Once your first book is printed, you'll never look back.[/ex]
	
look bad
	[p]v adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 выглядеть подозрительно, не внушать доверия:[/trn]
	\[L9 + bad) [ex]♦ I don't trust that man, he looks bad.[/ex]
	→ look good (1)
	[m1][trn]2 плохо выглядеть; быть непривлекательным на вид:[/trn]
	\[L9 + bad (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ I never wear yellow, it looks bad with my skin. I shan't buy that meat, it looks bad. You look bad, what's the matter? This cloth doesn't look too bad, does it?[/ex] → look good (2), look well (2)
	[m1][trn]3 казаться безнадёжным, не предвещать ничего хорошего:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (for)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The weather looks bad for sailing today. The results of the vote look bad for you.[/ex]
	→ look good (3)
	[m1][trn]4 выглядеть неприличным, неуместным:[/trn] 
	\[[com]IØ (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ It'll look bad if you're seen in a restaurant with another man's wife. Policemen are not allowed to drink on duty, partly because it might have an effect on their judgment, and partly because it would look bad.[/ex]
	→ look good (4)
	
look beyond
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 смотреть вдаль, выше (чего-л.), устремлять взгляд выше (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + beyond (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ If you look, beyond the trees, you can just see the farm in the distance.[/ex]
	→ see beyond (1)
	[m1][trn]2 заглядывать в будущее, просчитывать шаги вперёд, смотреть дальше:[/trn]
	\[[com]Т1 (no pass., usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Many young performers fail to look beyond the first stage of their training. It's a foolish businessman who won't look beyond the first year's profits. You have to look beyond these early difficulties to the hope of future success.[/ex] → see beyond (3).
	
look black
	[p]v adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [p]not fml[/p] казаться безнадёжным, беспросветным:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Whenever the future looks black, remind yourself of other difficulties that you have conquered in the past.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [p]not fml[/p] быть не в духе, быть в плохом настроении:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The director's looking very black this morning — who's been annoying him?.[/ex]
	
look blue
	[p]v adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 посинеть от холода:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Goodness, you look blue! Come in by the fire and get warm.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 иметь унылый вид:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ You're looking blue, what's the matter?[/ex] → feel blue.
	
look down
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 смотреть вниз; опустить глаза:[/trn]
	\[IØ + DOWN\] [ex]♦ When you're on a high ladder, don't look down or you might fall. Mary looked down to hide the shame in her eyes. Looking down at my feet, I saw the ring that I had dropped. [/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 быть расположенным выше чего-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (at, on) (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The church stands on a hill, looking down on the village.[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 смотреть вниз:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + down[/com]\] [ex]♦ Looking down the street, she could see the bus coming. Don't look down the barrel of a gun — you never know when it might be loaded.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 look down one's nose (at) [p]not fml[/p] смотреть свысока, сверху вниз, с презрением; пренебрегать; не одобрять:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ There has always been a tendency in England for high-born people to look down their noses at the lower classes. I wouldn't advise you to look down your nose at the offer, even if it is less than you expected.[/ex] → look down on (2).
	
look down on
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 быть расположенным на возвышенной местности, выше какого-л. места:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + DOWN + on (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The church stands on a hill, looking down on the village.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 смотреть сверху вниз, свысока; смотреть с неудовольствием, не одобрять:[/trn]
	\[T1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Women have grown tired of being looked down on by employers. She wouldnt let her daughter marry a boy from a poor family, as she looked down on him and thought he was not worthy of her daughter.
	The school looks down on such behaviour.[/ex]
	\[V3\] [ex]♦ At first, Mary's parents looked down on her marrying Jim.[/ex]
	→ look down2 (2).
	
look for
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 искать, разыскивать (что-л. или кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[IØ + for\] The police and the villagers are out in the woods, looking for the missing child. I spent hours looking for the ring that I had dropped. I'm looking for a suitable hotel; can you suggest one?[/ex] → quest for, search for, seek for
	[m1][trn]2 ожидать найти (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often continuous tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Many people who enjoy fast sports are looking for excitement- What results are you looking for? Some of the qualities that the employers are looking for in their workers are honesty, loyalty, and willingness to work hard. It's no good looking for help from that direction,[/ex] 
	—unlooked-for [p]adj[/p]
	→ search after, search for, seek after, seek for
	[m1][trn]3 esp. [p] old use[/p] ждать (что-л. или кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often continuous tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Are you looking for a parcel?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 [p]infml[/p] напрашиваться, нарываться на неприятность; лезть на рожон; особ. в выражении: look for trouble:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. continuous tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ If you drink and drive, you're looking for trouble. You're looking for a fight if you say things like that to me.[/ex] → ask for (3), head for (3).
	
look forward to
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]ждать, ожидать (чего-л.) с нетерпением:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I'm looking forward to some warmer weather after this bitter winter. The party, which had been greatly looked forward to, was ruined by the rude behaviour of an uninvited guest.[/ex]
	\[T4\] [ex]♦ We are all looking forward to seeing you again soon.[/ex]
	\[V4\] [ex]♦ Every year the children look forward to the holidays coming.[/ex]
	
look good
	[p]v adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 внушать доверие, располагать к себе:[/trn]
	\[L9 + good\] [ex]♦ I would never have believed such behaviour from such a sweet little girl, who looks so good.[/ex] → look bad (1)
	[m1][trn]2 хорошо выглядеть; выглядеть привлекательно:[/trn]
	\[L9 + good\] [ex]♦ That cake looks good — can I have a piece? You do look good in black; why don't you wear it more often? You're looking particularly good today: have you had some pleasant news? It'll better if it looks good, even if it doesn't work.[/ex] → look bad (2), look well (2)
	[m1][trn]3 сулить хорошие перспективы; выглядеть многообещающе:[/trn]
	\[IØ (for)) [ex]♦ The weather looks good for our garden party. How good does the voting look so far?[/ex] → look bad (3) 
	[m1][trn]4 выглядеть прилично, подобающе:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Whatever your excuse, make it look good. It doesn't look good if you drink too much. It doesn't matter if it isn't true so long as it looks good.[/ex] → look bad (4).
	
look here
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 смотреть в указанном направлении:[/trn] 
	\[[com]IØ + HERE (usu. imper.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Look here, where I'm pointing, not over there.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 слушать, обращать внимание:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (imper.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ "Look here," the teacher called to the children who were talking.[/ex]
	
look in
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 вглядываться; смотреть внутрь помещения и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[IØ + IN\] [ex]♦ When I drew back the curtains, there outside the window stood a crowd of people looking in.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 смотреть телепередачи по телевизору:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ I don't intend to look in tonight, there's nothing worth watching.[/ex]
	→ listen in (1), tune in (1)
	[m1][trn]3 заглянуть, зайти к (кому-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (on) (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'm glad to see you so much better; I'll look in again tomorrow. I thought I'd look in on you while I was passing. "Will 10 o'clock be too late to look in for half an hour?"[/ex] (Charles Dickens, [i]The Pickwick Papers[/i])
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 смотреть внутрь (чего-л.); смотреть в (книгу, зеркало и т. п.):[/trn] 
