	[m1][trn]1 двигать(ся) вперёд, продвигать(ся); идти впереди:[/trn]
	\[IØ + FORWARD\] [ex]♦ At last the line of people waiting to go into the cinema began to move forward.[/ex]
	\[T1 + FORWARD\] [ex]♦ We shall have to move some more of our men forward if the enemy keeps attacking all night.[/ex] → move down1 (1)., etc.
	[m1][trn]2 улучшать(ся), продвигать(ся); развиваться):[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ More and more newly independent nations are moving forward into civilized life.[/ex] 
	—move forward [p]n[/p]
	
move in
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 въезжать в новый дом; заселять:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + IN (to) [/com]\] [ex]♦ We've bought our new house, but we can't move in till the end of the month.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 + IN (to) [/com]\] [ex]♦ If the house stands empty for too long, the council will move a homeless family in. [/ex]
	—moving-in [p]n, adj[/p]
	→ move into (2), move out (1)
	[m1][trn]2 esp. [p]mil[/p] захватить контроль, командные позиции; вводить (новые силы):[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ As they moved in closer, they could see the moon shining on the enemy guns. Our competitors have gone out of business, so now our firm can move in.[/ex]
	\[T1\] IV/ten [ex]♦ is the best time to move the next group in, to deal with the enemy?.[/ex]
	
move in on
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 поселяться у (кого-л.) без разрешения:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Му brother's friend moved in on us without even as asking him.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 подвигаться ближе, приближаться:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The camera moved in on the table for a better view of the group. Even greater difficulties are moving in on the school system.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 окружать и готовить нападение на (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ The police are moving in on the criminals hiding in the house.[/ex]
	→ close in on
	[m1][trn]4 [p]not fml[/p] захватить контроль на (предприятии):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Criminals have been moving in on the taxi business for some years. The government may move in on the steel industry. [/ex]
	→ take over1 (2)
	
move into
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 вдвигать(ся); вводить (новые силы):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + into[/com]\] [ex]♦ Let's move into the shade, it's cooler there. As the funeral cars moved into the town square, the drums began to roll.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 + into[/com]\] [ex]♦ We'd better move some more men into that poorly defended position.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 въезжать в новый дом; заселять:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + into[/com]\] [ex]♦ What day do you plan to move into the new house?[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 + into[/com]\] [ex]♦ The council will move a homeless family into your house if you leave it empty for too long.[/ex] → move in (1), move out (1).
	
move off
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ move out (2)
	[m1][trn]отправляться, отъезжать, отходить:[/trn]
	\[IØ + OFF\] [ex]♦ The bus moved off before all the passengers had got on board. The line of soldiers moved off at a quick march.[/ex]
	
move on
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 направлять(ся) в другое место; оттеснять; отправляться (в путь):[/trn]
	\[T1 + ON\] [ex]♦ The police moved the crowd on when they threatened to damage the building.[/ex] 
	\[IØ + ON\] [ex]♦ It's time we were moving on, we've a busy day ahead. ‘Come on, sir, move on," said the policeman.[/ex] move along
	[m1][trn]2 передвинуть вперёд, продвинуть:[/trn]
	\[T1 + ON\] [ex]♦ Let's trick Mother by moving the hands of the clock on, so that she thinks it's already dinner time![/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 продолжать движение, идти дальше:[/trn] 
	\[[com]IØ (to)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Let's move on to the business of the meeting. I think we've talked enough about that subject; let's move on.[/ex]
	→ pass on1 (3)
	[m1][trn]4 продвигаться по (работе, службе и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (to)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Having done many years of school teaching, I felt that it was time to move on into college or university work.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 get a move on [p]infml[/p] торопиться:[/trn]
	\[[com] imper.[/com]\] [ex]♦ Get a move on, you two, we haven't all day to waste!.[/ex]
	
move out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 выселять(ся):[/trn]
	\[IØ + OUT (of)\] [ex]♦ No sooner had the family moved out (of the house) than the windows were broken and the paintwork damaged.[/ex]
	\[T1 + OUT\] [ex]♦ You'll be moved out by force if you don't pay the rent.[/ex]
	→ move in (1), move into (2)
	[m1][trn]2 отправляться в путь:[/trn]
	\[IØ + OUT\] [ex]♦ Blowing its whistle, the train moved out, slowly at first. [/ex]→ move off.
	
move over
shove over
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 отодвинуться, потесниться:[/trn] 
	\[IØ + OVER\] [ex]♦ There's room for three if you move over.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 уступить своё место, свою должность:[/trn]
	\[IØ\][ex]♦ Uncle left his position on the board of directors as he felt that he should move over in favour of a younger man.[/ex]
	→ ease up (3), move up (2).
	
move to
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 переезжать на (другое место); перевозить в (другое место):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + to[/com]\] [ex]♦ When did your family move to the North?[/ex]
	\[T1 + to) [ex]♦ When the new library was opened, all the books had to be moved to the new building.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 трогать (до слёз):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + to[/com]\] [ex]♦ The sad song moved the crowd to tears of sympathy.[/ex]
	
move towards
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 идти в направлении (чего-л.), двигаться к (чему-л. или кому-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + towards[/com]\] [ex]♦ A strange creature was moving steadily towards me.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 сближаться во мнении, во взглядах; близиться к пониманию, согласию:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The talks seem to be moving towards agreement at last.[/ex]
	→ move away (3)
	
move up
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 продвигаться вперёд или выше:[/trn]
	\[IØ + UP\] [ex]♦ Move up, there's plenty of room at the front. The line of people waiting to go into the cinema began to move up a little.[/ex]
	→ move down1 (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 also [p]not fml[/p] shove up пододвинуться, потесниться:[/trn]
	\[IØ + UP\] [ex]♦ There's room for three if you move up. Move up, will you, you're taking up all the space![/ex]
	→ ease up (3), move over (2) 
	[m1][trn]3 идти на повышение, продвигаться (по службе и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[T1 + UP\] [ex]♦ This student is finding the work too easy; she ought to be moved up into an more advanced class.[/ex]
	\[IØ + UP\] [ex]♦ it's possible to become a general by moving up through the ranks.[/ex]
	→ move down1 (2)
	[m1][trn]4 [p]mil[/p] продвинуть войска вперёд, ввести подкрепление:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Move some more men up, we need help here! Another group of soldiers moved up to take the places of those who had been killed.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 подниматься в цене:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The pound moved up a little today on the world money market.[/ex]
	
move upwards
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]расти, улучшаться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Sales moved upwards for the first time this year.[/ex]
	
mow down
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 косить, жать:[/trn]
	\[T1 + DOWN\] [ex]♦ We shall have to mow down the long grass in the big field.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 наносить тяжёлые потери, косить (пулемётным огнём и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The front row of soldiers were mown down by machinegun fire.[/ex]
	
muck about
muck around
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [p]infml[/p] слоняться без дела:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ “What have you been doing today?" "Oh, nothing, just mucking around."[/ex]
	→ mess about (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 [p]infml[/p] небрежно обращаться с (кем-л. или чем-л.); портить по небрежности:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Give те a straight answer, don't muck me about! Who's been mucking the tools around? Some of them are damaged.[/ex]
	→ mess about (4), etc.
	
muck about with
muck around with
	[p]v adv
	v prep[/p]
	→ mess about with (1), etc.[p] 
	[m1][trn]infml[/p] приводить в беспорядок, рыться в (бумагах и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Who's been mucking about with my papers? They're all out of order.[/ex]
	
muck in
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn][p]infml[/p] дружно браться за дело; делать что-л. вместе; водиться с (кем-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (TOGETHER, with)[/com]\] [ex]♦ If we all muck in (together), we'll soon get the job done. Do you mind mucking in with the other boys?[/ex]
	
muck out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ clean out (1), do out (1) 
	[m1][trn]чистить (конюшню):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Part of your job with the horses will be to muck out the stables every morning,[/ex] 
	-mucking-out [p]n[/p]
	
muck up
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [p]infml[/p] пачкать, грязнить:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I got my boots mucked up in the garden. Try not to muck up your new dress before the party.[/ex]
	→ dirty up (1), foul up (1), mess up (1)
	[m1][trn]2 [p]infml[/p] испортить (всё дело):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ If John mucks up his driving test again, I doubt if he'll ever pass it. Our travel arrangements have been mucked up by the ticket office.[/ex] 
	—muck-up [p]n[/p] → mess up (2), etc.
	
muddle about
muddle around
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn][p]not fml[/p] запутать(ся); сбивать с толку:[/trn]
	\[T1 + ABOUT/AROUND\] [ex]♦ Don't muddle me about like that, tell me exactly what you want.[/ex]
	\[L9 + ABOUT/AROUND\] [ex]♦ I've been muddling about all morning, and got nothing done.[/ex]
	
muddle along
muddle on
	[p]v adv[/p]
