play cool
	[p]v adj[/p]
	[m1][trn][b]play it cool[/b] [p]infml[/p] вести себя спокойно, не терять головы, сохранять спокойствие; не проявлять эмоций:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ When we made him the offer, he played it very cool so we thought he wasn't interested. There's no need to be nervous of meeting the Director; just play it cool and he'll probably appoint you because of your past successes.[/ex]
	
play down
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ play up (2)[p] 
	[m1][trn]not fml[/p] преуменьшать, умалять (серьёзность и т. п ):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The doctors thought it kind to play down the serious nature of her illness.[/ex]
	
play down to
	[p]v adv
	v prep[/p] 
	→ talk down to
	[m1][trn]заигрывать с (кем-л.), снисходить до (кого-л. ):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Politicians have to learn to play down to the voters, so that they seem to be ordinary worthy people and not someone too clever and untrustworthy.[/ex]
	
play fair
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 играть честно, соблюдать правила игры:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ He's a hard opponent to beat, but at least he plays fair.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 поступать честно:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (with)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The government is not playing fair with the taxpayers by refusing to declare details of public spending.[/ex]
	
play false
	[p]v adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]обманывать, подводить (кого-л):[/trn]
	\[T1b\] [ex]♦ I trusted my friend with my money, but he played me false and disappeared with it.[/ex]
	
play for
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 играть, исполнять пьесу и т. п. для (кого- л):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + for[/com]\] [ex]♦ Would you play that again for me? I'm playing this scene for a special friend who is in the theatre tonight.[/ex]
	\[[com]IØ + for[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'm still only a beginner on the instrument, it'll be years before I'm ready to play for the public. Jane was hurt that she wasn't asked to play for the wedding.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 играть, исполнять (музыку, роль) ради (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1 + for\] [ex]♦ She dreams of the day when she will be playing the piano for a living.
	\[[com][/ex]IØ + for[/com]\] [ex]♦ I only play for my own amusement, I've no intention of becoming a professional.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 играть [i] (напр. в карты)[/i] на деньги:[/trn]
	\[Т1 + for\] [ex]♦Never play cards for money.[/ex] 
	\[[com]IØ + for[/com]\] [ex]♦ Let's just play for pennies.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 [i] (в спорте)[/i] играть за (какую-л. команду):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + for[/com]\] [ex]♦ What team do you play for? All promising young cricketers would like to play for England.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 play someone for a fool esp. [p] AmE not fml[/p] принимать за дурака:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Stop this nonsense! Do you think you can play me for a fool?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 play for sympathy рассчитывать на сочувствие, сострадание, понимание:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The former Minister spoke only of his personal troubles, hoping that by playing for sympathy, he could draw the public's attention away from his mistakes, and be forgiven.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 play for time пытаться выиграть время; тянуть время:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ If we play for time on this contract, we may get a better price. Give me your decision now, stop playing for time! [/ex]→ stall for.
	
play forward
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ play back (2)[i] 
	[m1][trn](в крикете)[/i] сделать шаг вперёд, прежде чем ударить по мячу:[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + FORWARD (to) [/com]\] [ex]♦ If the ball lands near you, you should play forward (to it).[/ex]
	
play in
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 встречать (кого-л.) музыкой:[/trn]
	\[T1 (to)\] [ex]♦ The town band played the victorious team in to the dinner given in their honour.[/ex]
	→ play out (4)
	[m1][trn]2 встречать приход (нового года) музыкой:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ In this part of the country, a ceremony used to be held, during which pipers played in the new year.[/ex]
	→ ring in (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 play oneself in [i] (в спорте, особ. крикете) [/i]войти в игру, сыграться:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ This player has always been known for taking about half an hour to play himself in.[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 играть (где-л.):[/trn]
	\[IØ + in\] [ex]♦ It's healthy for children to play in the fresh air. The local farmer used to let us play in his fields.[/ex]
	\[T1 + in\] [ex]♦ Why are children not allowed to play ball games in the park? On Wednesdays, all our neighbours play cards in the village hall.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 исполнять, играть (роль, партию и т. п.) где-л.:[/trn] 
	\[IØ + in\] [ex]♦ A famous actress is to play in our next production. Many of the workers play in the factory's brass band.[/ex]
	\[T1 + in\] [ex]♦ He is perfectly suited to play Bottom in "A Midsummer Night's Dream". There is a part for every child to play in the school play. Jane is too nervous to play the piano in public.
	What an honour to play the drums in the town band! This piece is not suitable to be played in the open air.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 занимать (позицию, положение и т. п.) в игре:[/trn] 
	\[IØ + in\] [ex]♦ But I've never played in a defence position.[/ex]
	\[T1 + Zn\] [ex]♦ I think, we should play one of the younger men in goal. That man will not be played in any more games this year after his violent behaviour.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 play a part/role in играть роль в (чём-л.); иметь значение:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The director claimed that he had played a large part in the successful agreement. An important role was played by the radio in forming my interest in music. Modern drugs have a new role/part to play in controlling diseases of the mind.[/ex]
	→ have in2 (4), take in2 (9).
	
play into
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]play into someone's hands [p]not fml[/p] сыграть кому-л. на руку:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ By leading his men into a valley without another way out, the general played (right) into the enemy's hands. By admitting that he had not thoroughly studied the facts, the speaker played into his opponent's hands.[/ex]
	
play like
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 играть как (кто-л.), в манере (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + like[/com]\] [ex]♦ The pianist played like a man possessed by devils. All horn players wish they could play like Mr Brain.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 + like[/com]\] [ex]♦ He plays the instrument like a master. She played Queen Victoria like the old Queen herself. We should play this scene like professionals.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 play someone like a fish легко контролировать (кого-л.), управлять (кем-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ One of the secrets of the speaker's success is his ability to play his opponent like a fish.[/ex]
	
play off
	[p]v adv[/p]
	→ run off1 (8)[ex]♦ 
	[m1][trn](обыкн. в спорте)[/ex] проводить матчи, игры с целью определить победителя:[/trn]
	\[[com]Т1 (against)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The top teams in each group play off seven games against each other. The two equal winners had to play off an additional game to decide the winner of the whole competition. There was no clear result, so the organizers of the competition had to play off the two winners.[/ex]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ If the two top teams reach the same result, they will have to play off next week,[/ex] 
	—play-off [p]n[/p]
	
play off against
	[p]v adv
	v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 играть для выяснения сильнейшего:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + against[/com]\] [ex]♦ The top teams in each group play off seven games against each other.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [p]not fml[/p] натравливать (одного на другого) ради собственных интересов:[/trn]
	\[D1) [ex]♦ By playing off the Government party against the Opposition party, the smallest of the three parties was able to win votes and gain more seats in the House.[/ex]
	→ play against (2).
	
play on
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 продолжать игру:[/trn]
	\[IØ + ON\] [ex]♦ The cricket teams played on until it was almost dark. [/ex]
	[ex]♦ “Play on!" said the judge when the loose ball had been picked up.[/ex] 
	[ex]♦ “If music be the food of love, play on."[/ex] (Shakespeare, [ex]♦ Twelfth Night) [/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [i](в крикете)[/i] ударить мячом по своим воротцам:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (to) (often simple tenses) [/com]\] [ex]♦ A difficult ball came through; the batsman played on (to his wicket) and was out.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 (to)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Be careful not to play the ball on to your wicket.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 [i](в футболе)[/i] вводить в игру (игрока):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1b (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Smith accidentally played Sanchez on, so the Spanish goal was allowed.[/ex]
	
play on
play upon
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [i] (обыкн. о детях)[/i] играть чем-л. или где-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ + on/upon[/com]\] [ex]♦ Му children love to play on the seashore.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 исполнять, играть на (чём-л.):[/trn]
	\[IØ + on / upon I It's many years since I played on this stage. A man stood at the corner of the city street, playing on a drum.[/ex]
	\[[com]T1 + on/upon[/com]\] [ex]♦ This piece of music can be played on almost any instrument. Have you ever played Hamlet on such a small stage?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 направлять(ся); играть на поверхности (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]L9 + on/upon[/com]\] [ex]♦ The sunlight played on the lake, brightening the whole area. When I saw a secret smile playing on his lips, I knew that he was telling a lie. The wind was playing on the wrong side, and the ship could not make any speed.[/ex]
	\[X9 + on/upon\] [ex]♦ The firemen played the water on the front of the burning building, but could not control the flames. The policeman played his light on the objects in the room, but the stolen jewellery was not there.[/ex] → play along2, play over2
	[m1][trn]4 сыграть на (чьих-л. чувствах, вкусах и т.п.); воспользоваться (чем-л):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ By playing on the old lady's fears, the criminals were able to persuade her to give them her money. Politicians often win votes by playing on the electors' distrust of the party in power He made money by playing on the reading public's taste for violent stories. The plan can be defeated if you play on its weaknesses.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 play a joke/jape/prank on сыграть с (кем-л.) шутку, подшутить (над кем-л. .):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I know you're really awake, you're just playing a joke on me. Teachers often wonder what pranks the children will play on them next.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 play a trick on надуть, обмануть (кого-л), сыграть с (кем-л.) шутку (ради забавы или выгоды):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The firm won the contract unfairly by playing a trick on its competitors and not showing its intentions.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 play on words играть словами:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Much English humour consists of playing on words. [/ex]
	—play on words [p]n[/p]
	
play out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 играть на улице, снаружи:[/trn]
	\[IØ + OUT\] [ex]♦ It's too wet for the children to play out today, they'll have to amuse themselves indoors.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 доиграть до конца (пьесу, партию и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The tennis game was played out although the light was bad.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 разыгрывать; разворачивать сражение и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The battle for the leadership of the party was played out with much bitter personal hatred.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 провожать (кого-л.) музыкой:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The pianist played the happy pair out with the Wedding March.[/ex]
	→ play in1 (1)
	[m1][trn]5 провожать (старый, уходящий год) музыкой:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Gather round the piano, let's play out the old year with a loud joyful tune. [/ex]→ ring in1 (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]6 давать выход (чувствам, эмоциям и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ When children pretend to be violent, they may well be playing out evil in a lawful setting, preventing trouble in later life.[/ex]
	→ act out (2)
	[m1][trn]7 травить (что-л., напр. канат, верёвку); разматывать (верёвку и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Play out the rope a little at a time.[/ex]
	→ pay away (2), pay out (4), run out (5)
	[m1][trn]8 be played out [p]not fml[/p] устать; износиться; выдохнуться, исчерпаться; устареть:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Don't ask те to move, I'm played out. At the end of a busy year, the performers feel played out. That supply of money is played out, you'll have to try elsewhere. The idea that there is only one correct form of English is now played out. [/ex]
	—played-out [p]adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]9 play out time [i] (в спорте)[/i] не потерять очка:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ There's по hope of victory but at least we can play out time and perhaps not lose.[/ex]
	
