pull out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 вытаскивать, вынимать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1 + OUT (of) \] [ex]♦ Before I could see what he was doing, he had pulled out a gun. Putting his hand in his pocket, he pulled out a handkerchief.[/ex] → bring out (1), draw out (1), get out (5), take out (3), take out of (3)
	[m1][trn]2 выдёргивать, удалять, вытаскивать; выдвигать:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + OUT (of) [/com]\] [ex]♦ I shall have to have this tooth pulled out. Can you pull this nail out? I can't move it. He pulled the arrow out of his arm with his other hand. This tool is for pulling hairs out of your nose. Swimming against the current, he was able to pull the child out (of the river). You can pull the middle pages out of this magazine, to keep separately.[/ex]
	\[[com]IØ + OUT (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Will these pages pull out separately? This drawer is stiff and won't pull out.[/ex] 
	—pullout [p]n, adj[/p]
	→ take out (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 [p]not fml[/p] выдвигать (идею, предложение и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I hope you can pull out a better answer before the end of the meeting.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 вытягивать, удлинять (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Pull out the wire until it is very thin. The wool from the sheep has to be pulled out until it forms a thread.[/ex]
	→ draw out (2)
	[m1][trn]5 удаляться, отходить от станции, отплывать от берега; выезжать из своего ряда (для обгона):[/trn]
	\[IØ (of)\] [ex]♦ We reached the station too late, just as the train was pulling out. We should be able to pull out when the wind rises. Suddenly the car in front of us pulled out and nearly caused an accident.[/ex] → draw out (5, 6)
	[m1][trn]6 [p]not fml[/p] отступать, выходить из боя; выводить войска; отказываться от участия в чём-л.:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Jim saw that the firm was going to fail, so he pulled out before he got ruined. When will the soldiers have finished pulling out?[/ex]
	\[T1 (of)\] [ex]♦ British forces will be pulled out of the troubled area as quickly as possible. [/ex]
	—pullout [p]n[/p]
	→ pull back (4)
	[m1][trn]7 [i] (о самолёте)[/i] выходить из пикирования:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (of)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The plane was descending dangerously steeply but the pilot was able to pull it out just in time.[/ex]
	\[[com]IØ (of)[/com]\] [ex]♦ From the ground, we could see the plane pull out of its steep descent.[/ex] 
	—pullout [p]n[/p]
	[m1][trn]8 [p]not fml[/p] выходить (из депрессии и т. п.), возвращать(ся) в хорошее расположение духа:[/trn]
	\[IØ (of) (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Jim loses his temper easily, but soon pulls out (of it).
	\[[com][/ex]T1 (of) (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ You're feeling low because you're overtired, a good holiday will pull you out (of that feeling).[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 pull one's finger out [p] sl[/p] кончать волынить, сачковать:[/trn] 
	\[usu. simple tenses\] [ex]♦ The director wants that report by midnight tonight: tell the writers to pull their fingers out and get working![/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 pull out (all) the stops: 
	[m1][trn]а [i] (на органе)[/i] использовать все регуляторы для изменения или усиления звука:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ You should hear this great instrument with all its stops pulled out.[/ex] 
	[m1][trn]b [p]infml[/p] приложить все силы; сделать всё возможное, действовать всеми средствами; пустить всё в ход:[/trn] 
