	[m1][trn]4 [p]infml[/p] уволить, выгнать (кого-л.), часто незаслуженно:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ One of our best workers was pushed out to make way for the director's son.[/ex]
	→ thrust out (3)
	[m1][trn]5 push the boat out [p]infml[/p] шумно отметить какое-л. событие:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ After the concert, all the performers and many of their guests came to my home and we really pushed the boat out; the next morning, one of my neighbours complained about the noise.[/ex]
	
push over
shove over
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]опрокидывать, валить с ног (кого-л. или что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1 + OVER) [ex]♦ That boy, running round the corner, nearly pushed the old lady over. The wall is leaning at such a dangerous angle that it wouldn't take much effort to push it over.[/ex]
	
push round
	[p]v adv; prep[/p] 
	→ press round 
	[m1][trn]толпиться вокруг (кого-л.), налегать:[/trn] 
	\[IØ + ROUND/round\] [ex]♦ Newspaper reporters pushed round (the Minister), urging him to give them a statement. I could feel the cat pushing round my legs.[/ex]
	
push through
shove through
	[p]v adv; prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 проталкивать(ся), протискивать(ся), пробиваться:[/trn]
	\[T1 + THROUGH/through\] [ex]♦ The hole is too small; I can't push my arm through (it).[/ex]
	\[IØ + THROUGH/through\] [ex]♦ The crowd in the station was so thick that I had to push through (a mass of people) to reach my friend. The early spring flowers are just pushing through (the soil).[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [p]not fml[/p] оказывать поддержку (кому-л. или чему-л.); проталкивать (что-л. или кого-л.):[/trn] 
	\[T1b; DI\] [ex]♦ We shall need all the votes we can get to push the new law through (Parliament). His sister was a great help in pushing the student through (his examination).[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 push one's way through выбраться (из толпы и т. п.), расталкивая других; протиснуться:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The man got to the front in a most rude manner, by pushing his way through (the people).[/ex]
	→ elbow through, shoulder through, thrust through.
	
push to
shove to
	[p]v adv[/p] 
	[p]not fml[/p] 
	→ pull to1 etc.
	[m1][trn]плотно закрывать (дверь):[/trn]
	\[T1 + TO\] [ex]♦ Willyou please push the door to, it's not Quite shut.[/ex]
	
push towards
shove towards
	[p] v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 придвигать (что-л. к чему-л. или кому-л.); прокладывать (путь, дорогу и т. п):[/trn]
	\[T1 + towards\] [ex]♦ Не pushed the money towards me across the table, but I refused to take it. An old man pushed his way towards the front of the crowd. He pushed the letter towards me, forcing me to read the address. We seem to have been pushing our bicycles towards the next town for more than an hour.[/ex] → push at, thrust at (2), thrust towards (1)
	[m1][trn]2 продвигаться, несмотря на сопротивление:[/trn]
	\[T1 (no pass.)\] [ex]♦ Our army is still pushing towards the next village, under heavy enemy gunfire.[/ex] → thrust towards (2)
	[m1][trn]3 упорно стремиться к (цели, результату и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[T1 (no pass.)\] [ex]♦ All our efforts are pushing towards a reorganization of the prison system.[/ex] → press towards, thrust towards (2)
	
push up
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 поднимать, толкать вверх:[/trn]
	\[T1 + UP\] [ex]♦ The strength of the growing plants has pushed up the stones in the path. If you can't ride your bicycle to the top of the hill, you'll have to push it up.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 повышать, увеличивать (цены и т. п ):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Shops are по longer allowed to push up their prices whenever they want. This latest murder has pushed up the average to one a week.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 [p]not fml[/p] подталкивать сзади, наступать на пятки:[/trn] 
	\[[com]IØ (behind)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Competition is so fierce in the labour market that there is always someone pushing up behind you.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 push up the daisies [p]infml[/p] лежать в земле, кормить червей:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ I look after my health, I don't intend to be pushing up the daisies for a long time yet!.[/ex]
	
put about
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [i] naut (о судне)[/i] сделать поворот; лечь на другой галс:[/trn] 
	\[IØ (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The ship put about to pick up the man who had fallen overboard. To sail forward against the wind, we had to put about.[/ex] 
	\[T1b (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ If the wind changes, you'll have to put her about.[/ex] → bring about (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 распространять, распускать (слух, новость и т. п ):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often continuous tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Who's been putting about these stories concerning the government's secret intentions? It has been put about that several workers are to be dismissed.[/ex]
	→ go about1 (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 esp. [p] ScotE[/p] беспокоить, волновать; сердить (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Jim was greatly put about by the message. I am much put about by your recent behaviour. I hope I'm not putting you about by arriving unexpectedly.[/ex]
	→ put out (15), etc.
	
put above
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 класть, помещать, ставить (что-л. выше чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + above\] The notice was put above the door, and I didn't see it.[/ex] → place above (1), set above (1)
	[m1][trn]2 ставить на первое место, считать более важным и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[D1\] [ex]♦ Mothers are well-known for putting the needs of the family above their own interests.[/ex]
	\[V4b\] [ex]♦ Getting out of debt must be put above buying anything new.[/ex]
	→ place before (4), etc.
	
put across
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 ставить, строить (мост и т. п.) через что-л.:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + ACROSS\] [ex]♦ It will be easier to cross the river when the builders have finished putting the new bridge across.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [p]not fml[/p] излагать, формулировать, выражать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (to) (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Advertisements are intended to put across the best qualities of the product (to the public). How can I put such an unpopular message across (to the crowd)? The politician was able to put himself across (to the voters) as a suitable leader.[/ex]
	→ come across1 (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 [p]not fml[/p] успешно проделать, завершить (что-л.); «провернуть» какое-л. дело:[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ The director put the business deal across in record time.[/ex] → go through1 (3), put through1 (1), railroad through, ram through, take through1 (2)
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 класть что-л. поперёк чего-л.; строить мост через (реку и т. п ); перевозить на другой берег:[/trn] 
	\[Х9 + across\] [ex]♦ We must put a new bridge across the river to take the increased traffic. If you do that again, I'll put you across my knee and beat you! The bigger boats will be able to put more people across the river.[/ex] → go across2, etc., pull over2 (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 [p]infml[/p] обмануть, надуть (кого-л.), особ. во фразе put it/one/that across someone:[/trn]
	\[D1 \] [ex]♦ I put one across Father this morning: made him think it was my birthday, and he'd forgotten! The woman in the market put it across me by selling me some bad eggs. That class will never succeed in putting anything across the new teacher, he's too experienced. [/ex]→ put over2 (2).
	
put ahead
	[p]v adv[/p]
