	→ place in (10), put into (19)
	[m1][trn]25 put someone/something in the shade [p]not fml[/p] затмевать, превзойти; отодвинуть кого-л. или что-л. на задний план:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ We thought the first singer was very good, but the second one put her in the shade. Tom's earlier books were very popular, but have been put in the shade by his latest story, which is so much better.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]26 put a sock in it [p]infml[/p] заткнуться:[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. imper.[/com]\] [ex]♦ I've heard enough of your silly jokes, put a sock in it![/ex]
	[m1][trn]27 put a spanner in the works esp. [p] BrE infml[/p] испортить что-л.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Everything was going nicely with our plan, until my uncle refused to give us his money and put a spanner in the works. [/ex]→ throw in2 (4), throw into (9)
	[m1][trn]28 put a spoke in someone's wheel [p]not fml[/p] вставлять палки в колёса, мешать:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The children were about to steal the paints when the arrival of the teacher put a spoke in their wheel.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]29 put that in one's pipe and smoke it [p]infml[/p] примириться с (чем-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. imper., or with can[/com]\] [ex]♦ We refuse to go, so you can put that in your pipe and smoke it.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]30 put words in inverted commas esp. [p] BrE[/p] ставить слово в кавычки:[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ When you are writing your story, put all conversation in inverted commas. Many public speakers are so nervous of using informal expressions that even when they are speaking, you can hear that they have put them in inverted commas.[/ex]
	→ place in (11), set in2 (12)
	[m1][trn]31 put somcone/oneself in the wrong ошибаться; свалить вину на кого-л.; сделать (кого-л. или себя) ответственным за что-л.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Your mother put herself in the wrong with that unfortunate remark, and she should have the grace to say she's sorry. Why are you always trying to put me in the wrong? It's not my fault.[/ex]
	→ be in2 (30)
	
