	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 выдвигать (что-л.) вперёд:[/trn]
	\[X9 + FORWARD) [ex]♦ Why don't you set your chair forward, to get a better view?[/ex] → lay back, put back (1), put forward (1), set back (1)
	[m1][trn]2 [p] old use[/p] пускаться в путь:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ The climbers set forward towards the mountain in bright weather.[/ex] → go away (1), etc
	[m1][trn]3 выдвигать, предлагать или объяснять (идею):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The heads of government of many countries have set forward a better system for preventing world war. The committee's plans are set forward in the report [/ex]
	→ put forth (5), put forward (2), pull out1 (9), set forth (2), set out (8)
	[m1][trn]4 усиливать рост (чего-л.), продвигать вперёд (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (by)[/com]\] [ex]♦ This warm weather has set the crops forward by a month.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 переносить (событие) на более ранний срок:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ We shall have to set the meeting forward because of the holiday next week; can you all be here on Friday?[/ex]
	→ put off1 (4), etc.
	[m1][trn]6 передвигать (часы) вперёд:[/trn]
	\[X9\] [ex]♦ In spring, we usually set the clocks forward one hour, to take advantage of the summer daylight. My watch was slow so I set it forward three minutes. On my journey east, I had to set my watch forward to London time.[/ex]
	→ go back (7), put ahead (3), put back (8), put forward (6), put on (8), set ahead (3), set back (5).
	
set free
	[p]v adj[/p] 
	→ get free (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]освобождать (кого-л. или животное), давать свободу (народу):[/trn]
	\[T1bl [ex]♦ Someone has cut through the bars and set the prisoners free. Who will set the people free from these cruel rulers? The slaves were set free a century ago, but the black people are still not given their full human right.[/ex]
	
set in
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 вставлять (что-л. внутрь):[/trn]
	\[X9 + IN\] [ex]♦ Set the glass in carefully so that it fits the window frame exactly.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [i](обыкн. о плохой погоде или плохих условиях)[/i] наступать, устанавливаться:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ I'd like to get home before darkness sets in. An infection has set in, and his leg will have to be removed. You'd better paint the woodwork before decay sets in.
	\[It[/ex] + I3\] [ex]♦ It set in to snow at daybreak, and soon a severe storm covered the whole lakeshore area.[/ex] → settle in1 (2)
	[m1][trn]3 ставить, вставлять (что-л. куда-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The builders can set a cupboard in here, in the angles of the corner. This additional page must be set in at the correct point in the article. [/ex]
	—inset, set-in [p]n[/p]
	[m1][trn]4 [i] (в шитье)[/i] вшивать (рукава):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Can you help me to set in the sleeves? I've never found that particular sewing job easy.[/ex] 
	—set-in [p]adj[/p]
	[m1][trn]5 [i](в театре)[/i] добавлять (декорации), обыкн. в угол:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The scenery does not fill the space properly, we should set in an additional piece here. The set for the second act is smaller so we can set it in.[/ex] 
	—inset [p]n, adj[/p] → jog in
	[m1][trn]6 печатать с отступом от края листа:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The next three sentences should be set in, as they are someone else's words.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7[i] (o ветре или волнах)[/i] дуть или двигаться но направлению к берегу:[/trn]
	\[IØ\] [ex]♦ Don't leave your things on the sand, the sea is setting in and they could get wet. The wind set in strongly and we were unable to leave the shore.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 направлять (корабль) к берегу:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ It's time to set the ship in, we're expected in the harbour in an hour.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 [b]the rot set in[/b] [p]not fml[/p] ухудшаться:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ This used to be a peaceful city, but the rot set in when criminals came here escaping the law in their own country, and now it is not safe to live here. Since the ablest politicians left the party, the rot has set in and the public no longer have faith in their elected representatives.[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 вставлять, складывать (что-л. куда-л.), класть (что-л. или кого-л. куда-л.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + in\] [ex]♦ Be sure that the glass is set firmly in the window frame. Set the eggs gently in the basket. She set the baby in my arms and ran away.[/ex] → fit into (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 укладывать (волосы):[/trn]
	\[T1 + in\] [ex]♦ Why do you always set your hair in large waves, when it suits your face to wear it in a straight style?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 вставлять (драгоценности) в оправу:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + in (often pass.) I The diamonds were set in gold, making the ring very dear. The delicate model was set in plastic to protect it.[/ex] set with
	[m1][trn]4 печатать (что-л. каким-л. шрифтом):[/trn]
	\[T1 + in {often pass.)\] [ex]♦ This book is set in 10 point Times.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 сопровождать (сцену в спектакле определённым оформлением):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + in {usu. pass.)\] [ex]♦ The scene is set in a forest surrounding a mysterious castle.[/ex] → lay in2 (2)
	[m1][trn]6 [b]set foot in[/b] появиться, переступить порог:[/trn]
	\[[ex]♦ often simple tenses} The moment he sets foot in the United States he will be seized by the police.[/ex] → set on2 (12)
	[m1][trn]7 [b]be set in one's habits/ways[/b] иметь устойчивые взгляды:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Grandmother is getting set in her ways now that she is over 70.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 [b]set one's (own) house in order[/b] [p]not fml[/p] содержать свои дела в порядке, наводить порядок в своём доме: 
	[ex]♦ The chairman has declared that the department must set its own house in order before he will allow any additional money to be spent on machinery and paper.[/ex] → put in2 (16)
	[m1][trn]9 [b]set someone in mind of[/b] напоминать (о чём-л. или ком-л.):[/trn]
	\[simple tenses\] [ex]♦That tune always sets me in mind of a fine summer day. Her voice set me in mind of her mother. [/ex]→ put in2 (18)
	[m1][trn]10 [b]set something in motion[/b] начинать что-л.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ It may be a long time before you start feeling better, but at least we have set things in motion, and you can be sure of improvement in the long run.[/ex] → put in2 (19)
	[m1][trn]11 [b]set something in order[/b] приводить что-л. в порядок:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Help те to set the dining room in order before our guests arrive. When the doctor gave him six months to live, the first thing he did was to set his affairs in order, so as not to cause trouble for his relatives.[/ex] → be in2 (25a), be out of (19a), keep in (11), put in2 (21)
	[m1][trn]12 [b]set (words) in inverted commas[/b] esp. [p] BrE[/p] ставить (слово или слова) в кавычки:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Why do you set the name of the shop in inverted commas whenever you write it?[/ex]
	→ place in (11), put in2 (30).
	
set loose
	[p]v adj[/p] 
	→ get free (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]освободить (кого-л., животное и т. it.):[/trn]
	\[T1 bj [ex]♦ Someone has set the tigers loose from their cages and they are terrorizing the town.
	When we escape, shall we set the other prisoners loose?.[/ex]
	
set off
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 отправиться в дорогу:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (for,[/ex] on) [/com]\] [ex]♦ He set off for work, an hour ago, hasn't he arrived? The children were always excited to set off on a camping trip.[/ex]
	\[14\] [ex]♦ Having missed the last bus, we had to set off walking.[/ex] → go away (1), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 намереваться (сделать что-л.):[/trn]
	\[13 (usu. simple tenses) \] [ex]♦ I set off to make the dress by myself, but in the end I had to ask for help. [/ex]→ set out (3), start off (3), start out (3)
	[m1][trn]3 взорвать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Terrorists have been setting off bombs in Underground trains. The children gathered in the garden to set the fireworks off.[/ex] → go off1 (5). etc.
	[m1][trn]4 возбуждать (химическую реакцию), приводить в действие (какое-л. устройство):[/trn]
	\[Т11 [ex]♦ This instrument sets off an effect in the atoms of this substance, which causes it to produce power that can be made into electricity. The owner accidentally set off the fire bell, and two fire engines arrived.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 вызвать (что-л.), особ. ненамеренно:[/trn] 
	\[T1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The politician's speech set off violence in the crowd. Careless handling of international relations can set off a war.[/ex] → spark off (2), touch off (2), trigger off (2)
	[m1][trn]6 [p]not fml[/p] «завести» (кого-л.), «завестись»:[/trn]
	\[T1 (on)\] [ex]♦ If you set Father off on his favourite subject, he'll talk for hours. One amusing remark was enough to set the crowd off.[/ex]
	\[V4b\] [ex]♦ Don't set her off crying again, whatever you do.[/ex]
	\[T4\] [ex]♦ Once he sets off talking he never stops.[/ex] → start off (5), start out (4)
	[m1][trn]7 оттенять (что-л.), показывать (что-л.) с выгодной стороны:[/trn]
	\[T1 (simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The black cloth sets off the jewels nicely. The blue dress sets off the colour of Mary's eyes. The fine old house was set off by its formal gardens.[/ex] → show off (1)
	[m1][trn]8 выделять (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (in, by) (usu. pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The headwords are set off in black ink Spoken words are set off from the rest of the sentence by speech marks.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 уравновешивать:[/trn]
	\[T1 (against) (simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ Some of your debts can be set off by other money owing to you. At least there are good restaurants in this city to set off the disadvantages of the weather. We can set off the cost of the car against the saving in taxi rides. [/ex]
	—set-off [p]n[/p] 
	—offset [p]v[/p]
	→ set against (4).
	
set on
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 [p] old use fml[/p] двигаться вперед, выступать; атаковать:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (imper.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The enemy is near us; now, set on![/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 [p]not fml[/p] подстрекать (кого-л. делать что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] T1[/com]\] I[ex]♦ refuse to have that child to the party, he always sets the others on (to misbehave).[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 [p]BrE[/p] нанимать на работу (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Trade was so good that the firm was able to set on more workers.[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 класть (что-л. на что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]Х9 + on (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Set the plates gently on the table, they are very delicate He set his hand on my shoulder comfortingly. She set a finger on her lips to signal silence. Setting the child on his back, he continued the long walk home.[/ex]
	→ lay on2 (1), lay with (1), place on (1), put on2 (1), stick on2 (3)
	[m1][trn]2 ставить (что-л. на бок):[/trn]
	\[[com]Х9 + on (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ We had to set the piano on end to get it through the doorway. A title should be printed from top to bottom so that when the book is set on its side, it can be read easily.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 also set onto натравливать (животное на кого-л.):[/trn] 
	\[D1\] [ex]♦ I'll set my dog on you if you don't leave at once![/ex] → sick on
	[m1][trn]4 нападать (на кого-л.):[/trn] 
	\[T1 (often simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The youths set on the old man and robbed him. "She was set on by a thief in the park."[/ex] (BBC [ex]♦ radio news,[/ex] 9 September 1974)
	[m1][trn]5 be set on also be set upon решаться (на что-л.), настраиваться (на что-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Не seemed to be set on his aims. John is set on playing cricket for England.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 set one's cards on the table честно рас крыть спои намерения или высказать свою точку зрения:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ I see that it's time to set my cards on the table; I am interested in this affair because the woman in the case is my sister.[/ex]
	→ lay on2 (5), place on (3), put on2 (II)
	[m1][trn]7 set eyes on [p]not fml[/p] видеть (кого-л. или что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] often neg., simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Don't ask me where Jim is, I haven't set eyes on him for weeks.[/ex]
	→ clap on2 (4), lay on2 (7)
	[m1][trn]8 set someone/something on his/its feet помогать кому-л. или чему-л. улучшиться; поставить кого-л./что-л. на ноги:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ This medicine will help to set you on your feet after your illness. This summer's good trade should set the new business on its feet.[/ex] → put on2 (14)
	[m1][trn]9 set a finger/hand on [p]not fml[/p] трогать (ко го л.) или вредить (кому-л.): 
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Don't you dare set a finger on my son! If you set a hand on me I shall send for the police![/ex] → lay on2 (8)
	[m1][trn]10 set something on fire поджечь (что-л.):
	[ex]♦ The insurance people think that the owner himself set the building on fire, to get the money. One careless cigarette can set a whole forest on fire.[/ex] → catch alight (1), place to (3), put to2 (17), set alight (1), set to2 (5)
	[m1][trn]11 set something on foot [p]not fml[/p] начинать какую-л. деятельность, внедрять что-л.:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The director wants to set changes on foot to improve the sales.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]12 set foot on ступить (куда-л):[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ The moment he sets foot on American soil he will be seized by the police. [/ex]→ set in2 (6)
	[m1][trn]13 set one's hands on [p]not fml[/p] а получать (что-л.), доставать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ I'd like to know where I can set my hands on the special cloth.[/ex] → get on2 (8), lay on2 (11), put on2 (19b);
	[m1][trn]b схватить (что-л. или кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ The police will soon set their hands on the jewel thieves. I'd like to set my hands on the man who attacked my daughter.[/ex] → lay on2 (11). put on2 (19c), get on2 (9)
	[m1][trn]14 set one's heart/hopes on/upon [p]not fml[/p] возлагать надежды (на кого-л. или что-л.), связывать свои надежды (с кем-л. или чем-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Many parents set their hopes on their children for the future. Don't set all your hopes on this job, you may not get it. Jim has set his heart on winning the race.[/ex] → pin on2 (4). etc.
	[m1][trn]15 set one's/its mark on оставлять следы (на чём-л. или где-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ Years of suffering have set their mark on her face, lie has set his mark on the field of scientific enquiry with this recent study.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]16 set one's mind on настраиваться (на что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ If you set your mind on the directorship, you can get there in the end. The way to succeed in anything is to set your mind on winning.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]17 set something on one side:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а откладывать (что-л.) в сторону:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ She set her sewing on one side when the telephone bell rang.[/ex] → lay aside (1), etc.; 
	[m1][trn]b отложить (работу), особ. на время:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Tom set his new book on one side for a year while he wrote some magazine articles and informative material.[/ex] → lay aside (2), etc.; 
	[m1][trn]с отставить в сторону (различия, разногласия и т. п.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ It's time to set our differences on one side and work together for a common purpose.[/ex] → lay aside (3), etc.; 
	[m1][trn]d отложить (товар для покупателя):[/trn]
	[ex]♦ I'll set the goods on one side until you have made a decision about whether you want them.[/ex] → lay aside (5), etc.
	[m1][trn]18 set someone on a pedestal [p]not fml[/p] боготворить (кого-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ It's unhealthy in marriage to set your wife on a pedestal: it is better to accept her as a human being, with faults and failings as well as good qualities.[/ex]
	→ knock off (4), put on2 (34)
	[m1][trn]19 set a price on устанавливать стоимость (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ What price shall we set on this painting?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]20 set a price on someone's head устанавливать вознаграждение за поимку преступника: 
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ A price of $300 was set on the train robber's head.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]21 set the seal on завершить (что-л.), выполнить (план, замысел и т. п.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The politician formally opened the new theatre building, so setting the seal on the group's effort to be recognized.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]22 set one's sights on [p]not fml[/p] желать (чего-л.), нацеливаться (на что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ You can tell that the new committee member had set his sights on the chairmanship. Once he has set his sights on winning, there's no stopping him.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]23 set great/little store on ценить (что-л.) высоко/низко:[/trn]
	\[[com] simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ My grandfather set great store on his moral principles. [/ex]
	→ lay by2 (2), lay on2 (10), set by2 (2)
	[m1][trn]24 set someone's teeth on edge вызывать у кого-л. неприятное ощущение:[/trn]
