take in
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 вносить (что-л.) в помещение, относить (что-л.); приводить (кого-л.), отводить (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + IN\] [ex]♦ Take the washing in, it's raining! When the letters have all been opened, take them in to the director. Please take the children in, it's getting too cold out here. "May I take you in to dinner?" he said, offering the lady his arm.[/ex] → bring in (1), come in (1), get in1 (2), go in1 (1), send in (1), wheel in (1)
	[m1][trn]2 набрать внутрь (воды или воздуха):[/trn]
	\[T1a\] [ex]♦ It's good to stand on top of the hill and take in deep breaths of fresh air. The boat is taking in water![/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 принять (кого-л.) как гостя; предоставлять приют:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Now that his parents are dead, who will take the boy in?[/ex] → take into (2)
	[m1][trn]4 брать (жильцов):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Some of the local people take in students to add to their income.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 давать (кому-л.) работу в той же фирме:[/trn]
	\[Т1 (Го) \] [ex]♦ The firm may take you in just to please your uncle.[/ex]
	→ take into (3)
	[m1][trn]6 брать (работу) на дом:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Mrs Gardener has taken in washing for many years now.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 собирать (урожай, какую-л. сумму денег):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ If this warm weather continues, the farmers will be able to take the crops in a week earlier than usual. It costs almost as much to take in the new taxes as they will produce in income for the government. The shop takes in twice as much money every day as it used to. [/ex]
	→ be in1 (9), bring in (2), etc., come in (3), get in1 (5)
	[m1][trn]8 регулярно получать (газету):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ Which newspaper do you take in?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 замечать (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ It was amusing to see his surprise as he took in the scene.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 понимать (что-л. сказанное):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (simple tenses, nonassertive)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I listened to the speech carefully, but still I couldn't take it all in. Tell me your wonderful news again, I can't take it in! Could you take in what he was saying?[/ex]
	→ go in1 (7)
	[m1][trn]11 принимать на веру (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The poor man takes in all the lies she tells him.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]12 обманывать, надувать (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often[/ex] pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The salesman finds it easy to take in old ladies and persuade them to give him their money. Were you really taken in by an old trick like that?[/ex] 
	-take-in [p]n[/p]
	[m1][trn]13 ушивать (одежду):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I've lost so much weight that I've had to take in all my clothes.[/ex] → let out (8)
	[m1][trn]14 [p]naut[/p] убирать (паруса):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ When the wind blows hard, we have to take in sail.[/ex]
	→ take up (27)
	[m1][trn]15 присоединять (территорию), включать:[/trn]
	\[(usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ The British Empire once took in a quarter of the world. While in Italy, the tour takes in the famous old ruins.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]16 [p]AmE not fml[/p] посещать (места развлечений):[/trn] 
	\[T1a\] [ex]♦ What shall we do while we're here on holiday? Shall we take in a show?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]17 задерживать (преступника) и доставлять в полицию.[/trn] 
	\[T1 \] [ex]♦ Are you taking me in? [/ex]→ bring in (6), pull in (9), yank in
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 относить (что-л. куда-л.), проводить (кого-л. через дверь и т. п ):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + in\] [ex]♦ Would you take these cups in the kitchen as you're going that way? Let me take you in the back door so that the others won't see you.[/ex] → go in2 (2), take into (1)
	[m1][trn]2 везти (что-л. или кого-л. на автомобиле):[/trn]
	\[T1 + in) [ex]♦ I can take two of you in my car, if Jim will take the food in his car.[/ex] → go in2 (1)
	[m1][trn]3 добавлять (что-л. в пищу или напиток):[/trn]
	\[Т1 + in\] [ex]♦ Do you take cream in your coffee?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 держать (что-л. или кого-л. в руках или объятиях):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + in\] [ex]♦ Taking Mary in his arms, he kissed her fondly.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 поймать (животное в ловушку):[/trn]
	\[T1 + in\] [ex]♦ Do you take many small animals in these traps?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 ударить (кого-л. по чему-л.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + in (usu. simple tenses)\] The blow took him in the face.[/ex] → take on2 (3)
	[m1][trn]7 take delight/pleasure in получать удовольствие (от чего-л.):[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Old people take pleasure in warm sunshine even more than younger people. I don't see what pleasure the young people take in such loud music. I take such pleasure in watching the children grow up. [/ex] → delight in, rejoice at, rejoice in (1), rejoice over
	[m1][trn]8 take something in good part принять что-л. с юмором:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ The children played a silly trick on my visitor, but luckily he took it in good part and wasn't annoyed.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 take a hand in влиять (на что-л.):[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ The weather takes a hand in most of our plans.[/ex]
	 → be in2 (16), have in2 (4), play in2 (4)
	[m1][trn]10 take something in hand брать что-л. под контроль:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ The plans for the development of the city must be taken in hand by the council before too much building takes place without proper controls.[/ex]
	→ have in2 (5), be in2 (16)
	[m1][trn]11 take someone in hand брать кого-л. под своё начало:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ That boy will have to be taken in hand by the school if the parents can't control him. We ask each of you to take one new student in hand and show him how the library system works.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]12 take (an) interest in интересоваться (чем-л. или кем-л.):[/trn]
	[ex]♦ How long have you been taking an interest in children's rights? Jim took an interest in Mary from the first time he saw her. Jane takes no interest in her school work, only in her music. "Decent people do not take interest in politics, or elderly people in sport." [/ex](Ford Madox Ford, [ex]♦ The Good Soldier)[/ex]
	[m1][trn]13 take one's life in one's hands рисковать, часто безрассудно:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Those climbers are taking their lives in their hands, climbing the north face of the mountain in this weather. If you try complaining to the director while he's in a temper, you could be taking your life in your hands![/ex]
	[m1][trn]14 take someone's name in vain неуважительно упоминать о ком-л.; богохульствовать:[/trn] 
	[ex]♦ Who's been taking my name in vain? I do not hold such an opinion. "You shall not take the name of the Ijord your God in vain." [/ex](The Bible)
	[m1][trn]15 take part in принимать участие (в чём-л.):[/trn]
	[ex]♦ The whole school will take part in the concert.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]16 take pride in гордиться (кем-л. или чем-л.):[/trn]
	\[usu. simple tenses\] [ex]♦ You can take pride in your success, you earned it. The parents took such pride in the boy when he improved his behaviour. I take pride in always being on time for work.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]17 take refuge in укрыться (где-л.); прикрываться (чем-л.), прибегнуть (к чему-л. ):[/trn]
	[ex]♦ It's beginning to rain; look, we can take refuge in the hut. I sometimes think that perhaps she takes refuge in her illness to avoid her responsibilities. When I am sad, I take refuge in remembering happier times. He took refuge in telling lies.[/ex]
	
take into
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 прихватить (кого-л. или что-л. с собой куда-л.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + into\] Would you take these cups into the kitchen as you pass? Tomorrow I'll take you into town and we'll look at the shops [/ex]→ go into (1), take in2 (1)
	[m1][trn]2 принимать (кого-л. у себя дома):[/trn]
