	→ take out of (7)
	[m1][trn]10 снимать co сцены (спектакль), снимать с экрана (фильм):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The play was taken off after only three performances.[/ex] → come off1 (8)
	[m1][trn]11 убирать (блюдо) из меню:[/trn]
	\[T1 (usu. simple tenses)\] [ex]♦ We have taken the fish off, as it was not good.[/ex] → be off1 (6)
	[m1][trn]12 сбрасывать (вес):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I'm so pleased that I've been able to take off all that weight and get into my good clothes again![/ex]
	→ put back (3), put on1 (5)
	[m1][trn]13 снимать (цены, налоги и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[Т11 [ex]♦ Our prices are very good this week; everything has 10% taken off. The government has promised to take off the new tax as soon as possible.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]14 брать выходной день:[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ I'd like to take next Monday off to visit my sister in hospital.[/ex]
	→ get off1 (14), have off (4)
	[m1][trn]15 [p]not fml[/p] копировать, пародировать (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ The children do like to take off the new teacher, who has a noticeable manner of walking,[/ex] 
	—takeoff [p]n[/p] → have off (5), hit off (1)
	[m1][trn]16 [i] (особ. в крикете)[/i] убирать с поля (своего игрока):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ The captain decided to take Snow off and try a slower bowler.[/ex]
	→ come on1 (4), etc.
	[m1][trn]17 [i] (о плане, идее)[/i] получать развитие, иметь успех, успешно развиваться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ After a slow start, the plan soon took off and was accepted by all the directors. The circle of supply and demand should soon take off as the country's trade position improves.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]18 [p]not fml[/p] интересоваться, увлекаться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (on) [/com]\] [ex]♦ We must provide some really interesting new courses where the students can really take off. Many young people take off on loud music. [/ex]
	→ get off1 (17)
	[m1][trn]19 [p]AmE[/p] убить (кого-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (often pass.)[/com]\] [ex]♦ She was taken off by a rare tropical fever some years ago.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]20 take off the gloves to вступить в ожесточённую борьбу или спор (с кем-л.):[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Watch out for trouble when the director takes off the gloves to his Board![/ex]
	[m1][trn]21 take off one's hat to:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а приподнимать шляпу в знак приветствия или уважения:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ I knew that my father regarded те as a woman instead of a girl, the first time he took off his hat to me when we met.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]b восхищаться (кем-л.), снимать шляпу (перед кем-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. first person, present simple tense[/com]\] [ex]♦ You've done a remarkably fine job — I take off my hat to you![/ex]
	→ raise to (5)
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 убирать (что-л. или кого-л. откуда-л.); сниматься, убираться:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + off\] [ex]♦ Take your elbows off the table, children! let me take that piece of thread off your skirt. I'll take the books off the table so that we have room to eat.[/ex]
	\[[com]L9 + off (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The lid takes off the container to show the contents.[/ex]
	→ come off2 (1), get off2 (1), pull off2
	[m1][trn]2 снимать, сносить (что-л. с чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[D11\] [ex]♦ I can't take my boots off my feet, they're too tight! The wind took his hat off his head. I shall never take my ring off my finger.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]3 спасать (кого-л., обыкн. с корабля):[/trn]
	\[D11 [ex]♦ All the passengers were safely taken off the burning ship.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 убирать, исключать (что-л. или кого-л. откуда-л.):[/trn]
	\[D1 \] [ex]♦ Shall I take your name off the list? The policeman has been taken off the case.[/ex]
	→ come off2 (4), go off2 (1)
	[m1][trn]5 снимать (автобус, поезд и т. п.) с маршрута:[/trn]
	\[ D11 [ex]♦ The early morning bus will be taken off this route during the summer months.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 снижать (цену на определенную сумму):[/trn]
	\[D1\] [ex]♦ The shop is taking 10% off all dresses this week.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 take the edge off смягчать воздействие (чего-л.), притуплять:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ If you don! expect too much, it takes the edge off your disappointment. A piece of cheese will take the edge off your hunger. This small fire only takes the edge off the cold, it doesn't make the room really warm.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 take one's eyes off отводить взгляд (от кого-л. или чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ If you take your eyes off the children, they go wild! Can! you take your eyes off any pretty girl that passes? If I take my eyes off the music for even a moment, I lose my place.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 take the gilt off the gingerbread [p]not fml[/p] лишать что-л. привлекательности, убирать позолоту:[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Many wives enjoy the early years of marriage and then find that the hard work of caring for the children takes the gilt off the gingerbread.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 take something/someone off someone's hands освободить (кого-л. от чего-л.), снять (что-л.) с рук (кого-л.):[/trn]
	[ex]♦ We shall have to reduce the price if we want this buyer to take the house off our hands. I'd be grateful for someone to take the children off my hands for an afternoon.[/ex]
	→ have on2 (12)
	[m1][trn]11 take a load/weight off someone's mind/shoulders снять груз забот с чьих-л. плеч:[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Thank you for offering to organize the food, that takes (quite) a weight off my mind. A (great) load was taken off my shoulders when the children went away to school.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]12 take one's mind/thoughts off перестать беспокоиться, думать о (ком-л. или чём-л. ); выбросить из головы:[/trn]
	\[often simple tenses\] [ex]♦ Go for a walk or do something active, that should help to take your mind off your troubles. There seemed to be little that Grace could do to take her thoughts off that redhaired boy.[/ex] → have on2 (13)
	[m1][trn]13 take the smile off one's face [p]not fml[/p] огорчить, опечалить кого-л.:[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ It'll take the smile off your face when you hear the results of the competition that you were so certain of winning![/ex]
	[m1][trn]14 take the weight off one's feet/legs [p]not fml[/p] сесть и отдохнуть:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ After a whole day standing at work, it's good to take the weight off my feet when I get home.[/ex] → get off2 (14)
	[m1][trn]15 take years off [p]not fml[/p] делать (кого-л.) моложе, стройнее или счастливее:[/trn]
	\[[com] often simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ The doctor is so pleased about Jim's loss of weight, and says it will take years off him. Just seeing you again takes years off me.[/ex] → put on2 (42).
	
take on
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 взять с собой (кого-л. или что-л.) на борт корабля, самолёта и т. и.:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + ON) [ex]♦ Each passenger is allowed to take on one small suitcase to fit under the plane seat. Can we take the children on, or are they too young?[/ex] → take aboard
	[m1][trn]2 повести/повезти (кого-л.) дальше:[/trn]
	\[X9 + ON (to) [ex]♦ I When you get to the town, another bus will take you on (to where you want to go). This is as far as we go; you have to get someone to meet you and take you on.[/ex] → go on to (1)
	[m1][trn]3 набирать (пассажиров); заправляться (водой, топливом и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[Tia\] [ex]♦ The train stops here to take on water. We can't take on any more passengers, the bus is full.[/ex] → take up (11)
	[m1][trn]4 брать (на себя) ответственность, нагрузку и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ I think you've taken on as much as you can do this year, please don't start any other activity. The doctor says that Jim should take on much less heavy work.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 снимать (дом, квартиру):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ We've taken on a house in the country for the summer.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 нанимать (работника); брать (в ученики):[/trn]
	\[T1 \] [ex]♦ The factory has to take on 1,000 additional workers in order to complete the contract on time. Jane showed such promise as a singer that the famous teacher agreed to take her on, although she was so young.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 меряться силами с (кем-л.); соревноваться, бороться с (кем-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (at)[/com]\] [ex]♦ It was brave of you to take on a man twice your size. Would you take her on at tennis? Now that the government has defeated the employers, it can take on the unions[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 принимать (форму, выражение, вид); приобретать (какое-л. качество):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1a (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ His face took on the look of a hunted animal. These insects can take on the colour of their background, so that their enemies can't see them. His dreams took on the quality of reality.  As the word was repeated over and over again, it began to take on a different meaning.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 [p]not fml[/p] стать модным, популярным:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The new style didn't take on, so it was quickly dropped.[/ex]
	→ catch on1 (1)
	[m1][trn]10 [p]not fml[/p] сильно волноваться, расстраиваться; сердиться:[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (SO) (simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Mother did take on when she heard the terrible news. Don't take on so, he'll soon come back.[/ex]
	→ carry on (6).
	
take on
take upon
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 брать (кого-л. или что-л.) с собой в самолёт, автобус и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com]Х9 + on/upon[/com]\] [ex]♦ Each passenger is allowed to take one case on the plane, if it is small enough to fit under the seat. I shall have to take Jane on the bus, it's too far to walk to the music teacher's place. Taking the load on his back, he struggled forward[/ex]
	[m1][trn]2 везти на гастроли; брать на каникулы, в поездку и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + on/upon\] [ex]♦ The theatre company has decided to take three plays on tour. Do we have to take the children on holiday with us, or can we make some other arrangement for them?[/ex]
	→ go on2 (1), send on2
	[m1][trn]3 ударить (кого-л.) по (голове, носу и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[[com]Х9 + on/upon (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The blow took him on the nose.[/ex]
	→ take in2 (6)
	[m1][trn]4 take action on принимать меры, действовать согласно принятому решению:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Now that the committee has made its decision, how soon can the directors take action on it?[/ex]
	[m1][trn]5 take a chance/gamble on рисковать; воспользоваться рискованным шансом:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ I don't know whether we have enough money to last the whole holiday as we have planned, but I'm prepared to take a chance on it. You can put your money in a safe savings plan, or take a gamble on getting a higher rate of interest by putting it into high-risk shares. The train should have left by now, but I'll take a chance on its being late, and run to the station in the hope of catching it.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]6 take something (right) on the chin [p]not fml[/p] стойко принимать удары; держать удар:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Не had not expected to fail the examination, but he took it (right) on the chin like the man he is. It takes many years to learn to take life's blows on the chin.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]7 take a (firm) grip/hold on oneself контролировать себя, свои эмоции; хорошо держаться:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Take a firm grip on yourself, there's really not much to be afraid of. I had to take a hold on myself when facing the director for the first time.[/ex]
	→ get on2 (7)
	[m1][trn]8 take it upon one(self) to do something принимать, брать на себя ответственность за что-л.:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ You shouldn't take it upon yourself to judge all men. Don't blame me for your difficulties; you took it upon yourself to attempt this, no one forced you. If you have a child, you take it upon yourself to provide for his future.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 [b]take pity[/b]on сочувствовать, жалеть; сострадать; оказывать помощь (попавшим в беду):[/trn]
	[ex]♦ How can anyone fail to take pity on those children dying of hunger in distant lands? She prayed that God would take pity on her suffering and end her life.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]10 [b]take revenge on[/b]отомстить (кому-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]usu. simple tenses[/com]\] [ex]♦ The Prince felt it his duty to take revenge on his uncle for the murder of his father.[/ex]
	→ get even, etc.
	[m1][trn]11[b]take someone on one side[/b] отводить кого-л. в сторону для разговора:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Try to find а chance to take the director on one side after the meeting, and discover his real opinion. [/ex]→ draw aside (2), go aside, pull aside (2), take apart (1), take aside, take to (20)
	[m1][trn]12 [b]take a (firm) stand on[/b] твердо следовать своим убеждениям, твердо вести свою линию:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ The government decided to take a firm stand on admitting more foreigners to the country. The United States is now taking a stand on human rights.[/ex] → take over2 (7)
	[m1][trn]13 [b]take something on trust[/b] верить на слово:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ I can't show you anything to prove my story, you'll Just have to take it on trust.[/ex]
	
take out
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 выводить или выносить из комнаты, здания и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[Х9 + OUT (of)\] [ex]♦ The building is burning — take the horses out, quickly! Will you take the dog out for a walk, or shall I? Please take the children out (of this room), their noise is making my head ache. When the weather gets warmer, we'll take the chairs out and have tea in the garden.[/ex] → carry out (1), go out (1), etc., put out (2)
	[m1][trn]2 удалять, вырывать:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (of)[/ex][/com]\] [ex]♦ I shall have to have this tooth taken out, it's aching unbearably. Please help me to take this nail out (of my shoe).[/ex]
	→ be in1 (1), be out (1), be out of (2), come out (2), draw out (12), get out (2), get out of (2), have out (1), pluck out, pull out (2), take out of (2), yank out
	[m1][trn]3 вынуть, достать (из чего-л. или откуда-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1 (from, of)\] [ex]♦ Не took out а handkerchief and blew his nose. The thief took out a gun and forced the owner to give him the jewels.[/ex] → draw out (1), get out (5), pull out (1), take out of (3)
	[m1][trn]4 брать, забирать (откуда-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (of) [/com]\] [ex]♦ The library allows you to take out three books at a time. I shall have to go to the bank again and take some more money out (of our account).[/ex]
	→ draw out (11), get out (6), get out of (4), take out of (4)
	[m1][trn]5 удалять, смывать ([ex]♦ пятно, грязь и т. п ):[/trn]
	\[[com][/ex]T1 (of)[/com]\] [ex]♦ There's a nasty mark on the tablecloth, and I don't think the usual washing powder will take it out.[/ex]
	→ come off (3), come out (3), get off1 (4), get out (4), take out of (5), wash off, wash out (1)
	[m1][trn]6 брать (кого-л.) с собой в дальние страны:[/trn]
	\[T1 (to)\] [ex]♦ If you go overseas, will you take the family out with you?[/ex] → come over1 (3), etc.
	[m1][trn]7 брать (обеды и т. п.) на дом, из ресторана, кафе и т. п.:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 (usu. simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ I'd like three pieces of chicken to take out, please.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]8 получать (пропуск, лицензию и т. п ); получать (права) на (что-л.):[/trn]
	\[Т1\] [ex]♦ Did we remember to take out insurance on the contents of the house? Any person over the age of seventeen can take out a driving licence. The court may take out a summons against you.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]9 приглашать (кого-л.) в ресторан, па выставку и т. п.; светски развлекать (кого-л):[/trn]
	\[T1 (for, to)\] [ex]♦ How long did Jim take Mary out before they were married? I'd like to take you out to a meal on your birthday to give you a holiday from cooking.[/ex] → ask out, etc.
	[m1][trn]10 отстранять, выбивать (противника из игры):[/trn]
	\[T1\] [ex]♦ That was a clever move with your cards, to take out your opponent. It's your job to take out the other player so that members of your team can get control of the play.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]11 take the easy way out:[/trn] 
	[m1][trn]а найти лёгкий путь осуществить что-л.:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ I think I'll take the easy way out and give money instead of time.[/ex] → take out of (10)
	[m1][trn]b покончить с собой:[/trn]
	[ex]♦ Most people would be ashamed to take the easy way out, however bad their sufferings.[/ex]
	
take out in
	[p]v adv
	v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]esp. [p]AmE[/p] компенсировать долг (не деньгами):[/trn]
	\[D1 \] [ex]♦ I owe you $5 — can I take it out in beer and cigarettes? When the boy broke the window, the owner let him take it out in garden work.[/ex]
	
take out of
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 выводить (кого-л.) или выносить (что-л.) из (комнаты, здания и т. п); забирать (кого-л. откуда-л.):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + OUT + of\] [ex]♦ Take the horses out of the burning building! Please take the children out of this room, their noise is making my head ache. I shall take the children out of that school if what they say about their treatment is true. All the furniture was taken out of the house and left in the road.[/ex] → be out of (1), etc., pull out of (1)
	[m1][trn]2 вытаскивать, вырывать, удалять (что-л. откуда-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1 + OUT + of\] [ex]♦ Please help me to take this nail out of my shoe.[/ex] → take out (2), etc.
	[m1][trn]3 вынимать, доставать (откуда-л.):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1 + OUT + of[/com]\] [ex]♦ He took a handkerchief out of his pocket and blew his nose. The owner took a gun out of a drawer and pointed it at the thief. [/ex]
	→ draw out (1), get out (5), pull out (1), take out (3)
	[m1][trn]4 брать (деньги, книги и т. и.) откуда-л., где они хранятся:[/trn]
	\[T1 + OUT + of\] [ex]♦ Only three books may be taken out of the library at a time. I shall have to go to the bank again and take some more money out of our account.[/ex] → draw out (11), get out of (4), take out (4)
	[m1][trn]5 удалять, смывать (пятно, грязь и т. п.) с (чего-л.):[/trn]
	\[T1 + OUT + of\] [ex]♦ Will the usual washing powder take this nasty mark out of the tablecloth?[/ex] → wash off, etc.
	[m1][trn]6 снимать копию, воспроизводить:[/trn]
	\[X9 + OUT + of\] [ex]♦ His story isn't original, he took it out of a book.[/ex] → obtain from, etc.
	[m1][trn]7 снимать (транспорт) с графика движения, из расписания:[/trn]
	\[D1 \] [ex]♦ The early morning train will be taken out of operation during the summer months.[/ex] → come into (4), take off (9)
	[m1][trn]8 вычитать из (зарплаты и т. п ):[/trn]
	\[D1 (often pass.)\] [ex]♦ Unemployment insurance and income tax are taken out of your pay before you get it.[/ex] → stop from (2), stop out of (1)
	[m1][trn]9 [p]not fml[/p] отыгрываться на (ком-л.), давать полю (гневу и т. п.):[/trn]
