take over
	I
	[p]v adv[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 перевозить или переносить на другое место (обыкн. близко расположенное):[/trn]
	\[Х9 + OVER (to)\] [ex]♦ These bigger boats can take people over (to the island) in half the time. He took the letter over to the window to read it in a good light. Why don't you take the children over to your mother's more often?[/ex]
	→ come over1 (13), etc.
	[m1][trn]2 захватить власть; прийти к власти; взять управление в свои руки:[/trn]
	\[[com]T1a (from)[/com]\] [ex]♦ Military leaders have taken over the country. The young prince was allowed to take over the controls of the plane for a few minutes, to his delight. Our soldiers have succeeded in taking over some important enemy positions. Larger companies are taking over smaller firms by buying their shares,[/ex] 
	-take-over [p]n, adj[/p]
	→ move in on (4)
	[m1][trn]3 принимать (должность, обязанности и т. п.) у (кого-л. другого):[/trn]
	\[[com]T1a (from) [/com]\] [ex]♦ The government has promised to take over the responsibility for paying people hurt by criminals. I shall be glad when my daughter is old enough to take over some of the cooking. Who will take over the leadership of the party next time? If you rent this house, you can take over the furniture from the former owner.[/ex]
	[m1][trn]4 заступать на (смену, вахту, должность и т. п.):[/trn]
	\[[com]IØ (from) (often simple tenses)[/com]\] [ex]♦ The night nurse takes over at midnight. Thank you for your introduction; now I'll ask the next speaker to take over. Prices seem to go on rising whichever political party takes over. When do you take over from the former chairman?[/ex]
	II
	[m0][p]v prep[/p]
	[m1][trn]1 перевозить, переводить (кого-л. или что-л.) через (дорогу, реку и т. п.):[/trn]
